WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.050
- [Joe] One thing that-

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:03.803
- [Gary] You want a seat?

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:05.110
You want us to get you a seat, you sure?

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:06.250
We got it right there if you want it.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:07.250
- [Jimmy] Of course!

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:09.357
Thank you.

00:00:09.357 --> 00:00:11.000
Thanks.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.030
(Joe groans)

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:17.430
- [Joe] You ever see
John, the way he walked?

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.960
He used to bop along, with
a little bounce in his step,

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.700
and he had that big behind.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:23.730
- [Gary] Huge behind.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.992
- The brother's on the team
named "Chocolate Chip".

00:00:26.992 --> 00:00:28.239
(both laugh)

00:00:28.239 --> 00:00:29.072
- Makes sense.
- That never made it out

00:00:29.072 --> 00:00:31.400
- Of radio or anything,
- It was an inside thing.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.037
- The brother, you had "Chocolate Chip,

00:00:33.037 --> 00:00:35.950
"You got have a little black in you, man."

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:36.783
- A little something.

00:00:36.783 --> 00:00:38.650
- [Joe] "Chocolate Chip."
(crew laughs)

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:39.685
- And the afro, he was set.

00:00:39.685 --> 00:00:41.118
- [Joe] (laughs) He had
the afro, that's right,

00:00:41.118 --> 00:00:42.080
- He was set.
- To go along with

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.130
the double-booty, the big booty.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:44.916
- He was set.

00:00:44.916 --> 00:00:48.810
(crew chatters off-mic)

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.530
Anything you wanna talk about, Joe?

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:51.700
- [Joe] Anything you want to talk about

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:52.966
- You got it.
- Is fine.

00:00:52.966 --> 00:00:53.946
- You're set to go.
- We're good to go?

00:00:53.946 --> 00:00:55.328
What are we going live on?

00:00:55.328 --> 00:00:57.360
- [Engineer] Facebook and YouTube.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:58.690
- And the IG right there?

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:00.580
And are you live or not yet?

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:01.710
Any second?

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.350
And you're good?

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:04.430
- [Joe] Do I need to touch this at all?

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.027
- Nah, you're in great shape.

00:01:06.027 --> 00:01:07.530
(crew chatters off-mic)

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:08.363
What episode is it?

00:01:08.363 --> 00:01:09.540
- [Engineer] 311.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:10.373
- 311.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.890
Hey everybody, it's Gary Vaynerchuck,

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.790
and this is episode 311
of the Ask Gary Vee show,

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.563
and one that I will watch
in perpetuity forever,

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.580
easily one of the most
important men in my life,

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.033
without him fully knowing it,

00:01:28.517 --> 00:01:32.220
to every single code that
I ever had on my ATM,

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.300
on my passwords, several years
ago when I started realizing

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:36.540
more people knew who I was,

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.740
I had to go and change everything
from January 12th, 1969,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.850
'cause I felt that people
would eventually figure out

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.957
that those were all my passwords.

00:01:44.957 --> 00:01:45.790
- [Joe] (laughs) That's great.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.750
- Joe, it's a real honor to have you here.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.120
Obviously, my fanbase knows literally,

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:53.640
what you probably don't know
is I emigrated to this country

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.190
from the Soviet Union,
originally lived in Queens,

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.640
and I moved to Edison, New Jersey,

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.490
and the real way I got Americanized

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.040
was absolutely New York Jets football.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.040
My first season, back to John Riggins,

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.510
we were talking before we started this,

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.640
was the '82 season, which
was a great one for the Jets,

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.890
and, obviously, had they beaten Miami,

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.010
they would've played John
Riggins skins in that game,

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.570
and literally from that day on,

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:19.710
it has become the core thing in my life

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.770
is the New York Jets, besides my family,

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:23.780
so you can imagine how much
fun it is to have you here,

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:24.830
so thanks for being on.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.680
- Well thank you, thanks for having me.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.720
- So I have a lot of things
I want to talk to you about,

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.420
and the format of the show
is we're gonna do calls,

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.780
obviously, I've been promoting on social,

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.540
so a bunch of people are gonna
be super excited to call in,

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.910
I'm gonna start with a
couple of different things.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:38.850
I think, you know, the one
luxury of actually being

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:42.680
a hardcore Jets fan is that
I think I can take these

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:45.370
in different directions
than the normal stuff,

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.410
so actually, what I wanna talk about

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:50.340
is the season after the Super Bowl.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.870
A game that kind of still sits,

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:54.910
obviously this happened,
I was born in '75,

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:57.490
but the game against the Chiefs

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:59.940
that we lost in the
playoffs the next year,

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.420
I guess my first question would be,

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:05.010
was the team after the
Super Bowl a better team

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.030
than the Super Bowl team?

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:07.930
And obviously "better" is a funny word,

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.129
but did you think we were
gonna win Super Bowl IV?

00:03:11.129 --> 00:03:12.240
- I didn't necessarily think

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.260
we were gonna win Super Bowl IV,

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.550
but we felt like we were playing well,

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:19.830
and we ended up playing a team
that outplayed us that day,

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.980
the Kansas City Chiefs, man,

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:23.660
and you bring it up, Gary,

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.660
it was a windy, cold, funky day in Shea-

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.917
- [Gary] In Shea, yeah, we were home.

00:03:29.917 --> 00:03:31.931
- We were home,

00:03:31.931 --> 00:03:34.450
and we got held to six points, all right?

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:35.283
- [Gary] I'm aware.

00:03:35.283 --> 00:03:38.650
- Bobby Bell, they had four or five

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.020
defensive players in the
Hall of Fame now, you know,

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.860
I mean, they were good,

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.080
and I remember a play that,

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.170
I actually thought about
trying to fool Bobby Bell,

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:49.730
during the week when
we were getting ready,

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:51.740
and I made a big mistake.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:53.810
You don't fool Bobby Bell, you know,

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:56.060
a great linebacker that he was,

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.323
and we only scored six
points, they beat us, man.

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.210
- When I bring up that game, right,

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.890
what are the biggest things
that you think about?

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.410
Did that game stick with you?

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:09.030
Because obviously, and some of
this stuff I might get wrong,

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.040
because obviously I wasn't around,

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.440
but the next season, you got hurt, right?

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:14.721
Because next season was
a 4-11 season, right?

00:04:14.721 --> 00:04:15.953
- Yeah.
- And it was like,

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.770
I don't know you well
enough to know, you know,

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:20.950
is it stuff you think about?

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:21.783
Is it not?

00:04:21.783 --> 00:04:23.650
But that season, for some reason,

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:25.350
I don't know why it sticks, obviously,

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:27.573
everybody talks about the prior season,

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.180
and Len Dawson.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:31.370
What kind of player was Len Dawson?

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.230
- Lenny Dawson was sweet, man.

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.150
I mean, he was sweet, he was smooth.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:37.277
I tried to run like Lenny Dawson.

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:38.614
- (laughs) The knees
- I'm talking about

00:04:38.614 --> 00:04:39.781
- Wouldn't let you.
- Jogging, on and off

00:04:39.781 --> 00:04:41.158
- He was a good ball player.
- the field, you know.

00:04:41.158 --> 00:04:44.260
- I was impressed with his
mannerisms, his coolness,

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.500
he was a real gentleman,

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.340
and he was brilliant as a player.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.360
- But that game, that season,

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.193
did that hurt for a long time?

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:54.750
You had so much going on
in your life at that point,

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.850
obviously, the prior season
puts you on a pedestal,

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:00.620
in this city, the biggest
city in the world,

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:02.790
are you the kind of guy
that after that game,

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.590
you're devastated for 24 hours?

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:05.900
You think about it for a month?

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.540
Or you go down to Miami and have fun?

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:08.600
What kind of guy were you?

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:10.150
I'm very curious what happens

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:12.030
after that level of adversity,

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.370
I assume we're at home,

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:16.030
the Raiders were a really
good team that year too,

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:18.000
so you have to face those
guys again if you win,

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.000
but were you upset?

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:21.340
Or were you kind of like,

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.363
you understood that they outplayed you,

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:26.830
I'm trying to get into the mindset of

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:28.100
you're walking off the field,

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.860
and then what happens the next 30 days.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.510
- Gary, I'll share with you

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:34.050
what my head coach

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:35.730
told my freshman class

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.650
of football players at
the University of Alabama

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:40.810
the first time we met our head coach

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:45.210
and had a meeting with him,
Coach Paul "Bear" Bryant.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:47.540
He stood up in front of us and he said

00:05:48.407 --> 00:05:53.010
"I'm gonna teach y'all how to
keep from beating yourselves."

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:55.000
Now, I came from PA, man,

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:56.935
it's tough for me to understand him,

00:05:56.935 --> 00:05:58.050
(Gary laughs)
and we won a championship

00:05:58.050 --> 00:05:59.360
back there in Beaver Falls,

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.080
and that sounded to me like, what,

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.547
playing conservative or playing scared?

00:06:03.547 --> 00:06:07.560
"I'm gonna teach y'all how to
keep from beating yourselves."

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:12.560
And then he said "You're
gonna remember the losses.

00:06:12.717 --> 00:06:14.537
"The mistakes.

00:06:14.537 --> 00:06:17.447
"The games you didn't play well in

00:06:17.447 --> 00:06:19.747
"more than you're gonna
remember the good games,

00:06:19.747 --> 00:06:22.500
"even the big games you've won."

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:26.190
To this day, you bring up
the Kansas City Chiefs game

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:27.979
in 1969,

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:29.200
I promise you,

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:31.944
that's one of the ones
we're talking about,

00:06:31.944 --> 00:06:33.410
- Stacked state.
- Coming from Coach Bryant.

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:37.350
Losing to Texas my senior
year in the Orange Bowl,

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:39.180
that jumps up at me.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.180
Losing once to that team
from across Alabama State,

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:46.329
A-Burn, I called them, for most folks

00:06:46.329 --> 00:06:47.970
(Gary laughs)
but losing to them,

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.250
that gets me right here to this day.

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.550
Sure, we won the championship, man,

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:54.570
and I mentioned high school, college,

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:58.700
the goal for every player should
be to make the team own up,

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:00.970
you gotta be good enough
to get on any team,

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:02.680
whether it's little league baseball,

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:04.010
junior high basketball,

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:06.200
you gotta make the team,

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:08.610
and the next goal should
be to win the championship.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:09.443
- [Gary] That's right.

00:07:09.443 --> 00:07:11.240
- And we did it on those levels,

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:13.100
and I'm thrilled about that,

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:14.900
but I think of the games I played

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:16.640
like a bum first.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:18.630
- Do you think you played
like a bum in that game?

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:19.463
- In which game?

00:07:19.463 --> 00:07:20.990
The Kansas City Chiefs game?

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:22.160
- I know there's four picks in that game,

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.840
but, you know, real football fans know

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:25.930
some of those are on the quarterback,

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:26.970
and some of them are not.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:29.588
- And some are on a good defensive play-

00:07:29.588 --> 00:07:30.421
- Or somebody running
- And they can make excuses

00:07:30.421 --> 00:07:32.194
- A wrong round
- about the hawk,

00:07:32.194 --> 00:07:33.180
- Or something silly, sure.
- All that stuff,

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:34.060
- Missed a hawk
- They're talking about

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.010
the hawk, the wind, you know.

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:38.360
But no, I mean, I didn't play well.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.840
Had I played well, we would've
possibly won the game.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:42.690
You know what?

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:45.300
That triggers a thought
when I think about it,

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:46.133
Tom Brady.

00:07:46.133 --> 00:07:48.380
I think about Tom Brady
in this day and age,

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:50.780
and after watching him over the years,

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:53.710
I know I literally lost a game

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:57.170
or more when I played for the Jets.

00:07:57.170 --> 00:07:59.830
I haven't seen Tom Bray in 18 years,

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:01.990
or since he's been
playing for the Patriots,

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:03.730
cost them a game.

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:04.563
- Well, you know, it's funny,

00:08:04.563 --> 00:08:05.880
because the playoff game
that we went up there

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:06.740
and beat them on,

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.980
I thought the pass that he threw

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:10.930
that David Harris intercepted

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.270
changed the complete
complexity of that game

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:14.600
throughout the rest of the game,

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:16.520
so I'll chalk that up to Tom Brady's loss.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:17.740
- Okay, that's one play.

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:19.541
- I'll take it, I'll take it though.

00:08:19.541 --> 00:08:20.374
(Joe laughs)
I want it.

00:08:20.374 --> 00:08:21.207
Listen, I'm hanging on here, Joe.

00:08:21.207 --> 00:08:22.997
- Okay, that's right.
- As a diehard Jets fan,

00:08:22.997 --> 00:08:26.950
that name is tough to deal
with, as you can imagine.

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:27.990
Let me ask you another question

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:30.013
that I was really curious about.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:32.180
By the way, everybody who's watching,

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:34.430
you put in calls, I'm gonna talk to Joe

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:37.630
a lot about marketing, because
I think what Sonny did,

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:39.160
and we'll get into this,

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:42.223
I'm very curious from your
perspective of being the human.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.490
There is no question in my mind,

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:47.810
my brother A.J. and I started
this marketing company,

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:51.210
and several years ago, he
left to follow his passion,

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:53.260
and my brother is in the football

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.040
sports representation business,

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:56.470
so he's a football agent,

00:08:56.470 --> 00:08:58.430
and obviously we come from
a marketing background,

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:01.040
so you can imagine I'm very affected

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:04.670
with the way you became who
you were in the public eye,

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:07.490
and that wasn't just who you were

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:10.410
as a charismatic, successful player,

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.190
it was the people around you

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.010
that were helping you make decisions

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:17.500
on brand deals, the SI cover is all-time,

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:19.710
the pantyhose thing is all-time,

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:21.670
there's a lot of things that were done

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:23.270
between you and your team

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:26.904
that has created the
legendary status that you are,

00:09:26.904 --> 00:09:29.940
obviously the game is so
disproportionately powerful,

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:31.160
but I don't think people understand

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:33.040
how a full thing is usually built,

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:34.594
so for everybody who's watching,

00:09:34.594 --> 00:09:35.760
because I know marketing
and entrepreneurship

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:37.860
is such a big part of me and this show,

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:38.850
we'll get to that,

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:39.850
you're gonna have to stick with me,

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:42.020
because I'm going very nerd football here,

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:44.280
because I've gotta see
it through for myself,

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:45.530
I'm very curious,

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:46.913
and I actually don't have as
much knowledge about this,

00:09:46.913 --> 00:09:50.810
because this is the period where
I don't know as much about,

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:52.220
Richard Todd.

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:53.270
This is very interesting to me,

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:55.340
because the '82 season is my first season,

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:57.010
and he was the Jets
quarterback at that time,

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:58.833
so he was my first Jets quarterback,

00:10:00.155 --> 00:10:01.900
when I look at the history
books and read stuff,

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:04.390
he comes into the team with all this fame

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:05.630
and things of that nature,

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:08.740
there's a story I can't
recall about something,

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:11.620
you said some amazing joke to him

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:13.190
at a training camp or something,

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:14.760
I don't remember the whole story,

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:16.400
and I might even be getting it wrong,

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:19.530
I'm curious, as a nerdy, nerdy Jets fan,

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:22.250
where were you at the
point in your career,

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:24.880
what was your relationship like with him,

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:27.520
when I say that name, give
me a story or two that

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:30.240
maybe you haven't told, or
give me a scoop here, Joe,

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:32.240
tell me something about
you and Richard Todd.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.590
- Every year that I played pro football,

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:36.460
the 13 years that I played,

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:38.370
I would go back to
Tuscaloosa, Alabama, man.

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.050
- Every year.
- To train,

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:41.591
- I didn't know that.
- That's right,

00:10:41.591 --> 00:10:44.012
that's where I'd go do
my training, I'd go back-

00:10:44.012 --> 00:10:45.000
- Joe, I'm going to interrupt
you, and I apologize,

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:46.740
and everyone's laughing now
because I love to do it,

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:47.913
as a PA boy,

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:52.150
obviously I've seen you
talk about it a lot,

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:54.420
coach down there and that scene,

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.540
that was really transformational for you,

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:57.700
that time down there, huh?

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:00.260
- It was, I didn't even visit the campus

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:01.810
before I went there.

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:06.140
I didn't know Coach Paul
"Bear" Bryant at the time.

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:09.400
I saw Alabama play one
game before I went there,

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:10.890
and that was in the Liberty Bowl

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:12.863
on a black-and-white television,

00:11:13.911 --> 00:11:16.500
and I really didn't
know anything about it.

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:18.810
- Who came in second for Joe Namath?

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:19.940
- Second where?

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:22.260
- If you didn't go to 'Bama,
where would've you gone?

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:24.560
- Maryland, University
of Maryland had I passed

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:27.130
the college boards, but I
didn't score high enough

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:27.963
- You flat-out wanted
- On the college boards-

00:11:27.963 --> 00:11:29.280
- to go to Maryland.

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:30.783
- No, I didn't want to go to college,

00:11:30.783 --> 00:11:32.620
(laughs)
I wanted to play baseball.

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:33.453
- [Gary] Is that true?

00:11:33.453 --> 00:11:37.180
- I had the opportunity to
sign a baseball contract,

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:41.010
but at home, that was at
a time where you still did

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:42.650
what your mother, your father said,

00:11:42.650 --> 00:11:47.020
and my father and mother
had divorced early on,

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:49.830
not so early, but it was early in my life,

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:52.070
and now it was time to decide

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:55.270
what the youngest in the
family was going to do,

00:11:55.270 --> 00:11:58.250
go to college or sign
the baseball contract,

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:00.420
and so we had a meeting at the house.

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:02.510
My oldest brother ran the meeting,

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:05.617
my mother was sitting
there, and brother Bob said,

00:12:05.617 --> 00:12:07.157
"Well, mom," he turned to her, he said

00:12:07.157 --> 00:12:09.200
"Mom, what do you want Joe to do?"

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:10.703
And my mother said "Oh,

00:12:11.687 --> 00:12:13.874
"I want Joey to go to college."

00:12:13.874 --> 00:12:15.160
(Gary laughs)

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:16.593
Bob hit the table-

00:12:16.593 --> 00:12:17.567
(pounds table)
"That's it,

00:12:17.567 --> 00:12:18.790
"you're going to college."

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:20.010
- That was it.
- That was it!

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:21.960
- [Gary] And were you pissed?

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:23.670
- I was crushed.
(Gary laughs)

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:25.410
I couldn't be too angry

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:28.784
because Bob could still
beat me up then, you know-

00:12:28.784 --> 00:12:30.103
- But were you really disappointed?

00:12:30.103 --> 00:12:34.011
- Man, I had a brand new
Starfire Pontiac Convertible

00:12:34.011 --> 00:12:35.060
(Gary laughs)
picked out, man,

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:38.100
powder blue with a chrome
strip along the side,

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:39.210
a white top,

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:42.705
50,000 dollar bonus in 1961-

00:12:42.705 --> 00:12:43.860
- Which is like
= Which is pretty cool.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:44.797
- A trillion dollars.
- Yeah man,

00:12:44.797 --> 00:12:46.410
are you kidding?

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:48.330
I loved the baseball.

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:51.800
I'd run around and catch and
bat like Roberto Clemente,

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:53.590
or tried to, you know,

00:12:53.590 --> 00:12:56.590
I really loved baseball, but-

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:57.840
- [Gary] Mom wanted you to go to college.

00:12:57.840 --> 00:12:58.911
- That was that.

00:12:58.911 --> 00:13:00.230
(stammers)

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:03.200
- And then Maryland hit your radar-

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:04.610
- Maryland-
- Why?

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:06.720
- I liked Tom Nugent, the coach there,

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:09.130
I liked his style of
football, they threw it a lot,

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:13.260
he started the I-formation
when he was at Florida State,

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:16.052
and he was just a charismatic guy.

00:13:16.052 --> 00:13:18.040
- And Joe, this was not an
era where a big prospect

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:20.360
like Joe Namath with
not passing the boards,

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:23.060
like Maryland couldn't
figure out some funny,

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:25.240
that wasn't the ecosystem in
college football back then?

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:26.210
- It didn't work that way,

00:13:26.210 --> 00:13:29.644
and what happened is Coach
Nugent called Coach Bryant-

00:13:29.644 --> 00:13:31.100
- Interesting, interesting.
- And let him know that

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:33.280
I was still loose.

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:37.410
And unbeknownst to me, I didn't
know Howard Schnellenberger,

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:39.990
an Assistant Coach at
Alabama at that time,

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:42.360
showed up at our house in Beaver Falls,

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:44.210
there was a knock at the door,

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:46.740
and he came in and talked to my mother.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:48.130
- And that was that.
- She went upstairs,

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:51.040
packed me a suitcase you
could put under the front seat

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:54.060
of this, you know, a bus
seat, now on a plane,

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.390
it was that big, came downstairs,

00:13:56.390 --> 00:13:57.750
gave me a five dollar bill,

00:13:57.750 --> 00:13:59.217
and looked at Coach
Schnellenberger and said

00:13:59.217 --> 00:14:00.440
"Take him."

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:04.040
And that was it, man, I was
off to Tuscaloosa, Alabama,

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:06.966
I never heard of Tuscaloosa, Alabama

00:14:06.966 --> 00:14:07.866
(Gary laughs)
before that.

00:14:07.866 --> 00:14:08.870
- [Gary] What about
Alabama itself? (laughs)

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:11.240
- Well I did see them
play in the Liberty Bowl,

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:12.760
I liked their style, man,

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:15.762
they had dark headgears and
white uniforms that day,

00:14:15.762 --> 00:14:16.595
- That would work for you.
- I remember,

00:14:16.595 --> 00:14:19.160
and I caught myself pulling
for them, but they lost.

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:20.910
- And so, back to the story,

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:22.910
so you go down, and Todd is down there,

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:24.230
one of those off-seasons.

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:28.010
- Well, you see, I was, again,
already playing pro ball,

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:30.203
but I'd go back to train,

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:35.620
May, June, early June,
before our training camp,

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:38.410
and Richard was the quarterback at Alabama

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:42.930
one of those years, and they
were running a wishbone offense

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:44.450
when he was a senior.

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:46.340
But when he was younger player,

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:48.210
before they put the wishbone in,

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:51.560
Richard was still running
sprint-out stuff and throwing.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:53.560
So I used to work with Richard

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:55.710
specifically on dropping back,

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:59.090
the straight drop-back
pass, he had a strong arm,

00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:00.020
and he was good,

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:03.460
he was a big heart, good teammate, man,

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:05.220
you'd like everything about Richard,

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:07.510
except that day against Miami, maybe,

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:09.480
down there in the bad weather with

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:10.819
- [Both] A.J. Dewey.

00:15:10.819 --> 00:15:12.669
(laughs)

00:15:12.669 --> 00:15:13.806
- That one hurts.
- I know.

00:15:13.806 --> 00:15:14.960
- That's the first time I cried.

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:16.950
Only the '86

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:18.010
Browns loss

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:19.870
made me cry harder,

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:21.570
but that was a tough one.

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:23.910
- Yeah, well, you know,

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:25.300
some are tougher than others,

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:26.800
- Yep, yep.
- They all stink, you know,

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:28.740
you never get tired of winning,

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:31.970
two things in life we don't
get tired of, staying healthy,

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:32.803
- [Both] And winning.

00:15:32.803 --> 00:15:33.636
- [Gary] For sure.

00:15:33.636 --> 00:15:35.246
- You know, your girlfriend
will run you off,

00:15:35.246 --> 00:15:37.080
or you'll wanna run her off sometimes.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:38.871
- [Gary] For sure, winning and health.

00:15:38.871 --> 00:15:39.704
- The two things that are steady

00:15:39.704 --> 00:15:40.537
are good health and winning.

00:15:40.537 --> 00:15:43.030
- So did you tell the Jets
that you thought that,

00:15:43.030 --> 00:15:44.190
was there ever a conversation

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:45.840
between you and the
front office about Todd?

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:49.437
- There might've been, because
we drafted him number one

00:15:49.437 --> 00:15:53.270
in '76, we drafted Richard number one.

00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:56.040
And Richard had great
training with Coach Bryant,

00:15:56.040 --> 00:16:00.843
I mean he was a fine
player and strong, but-

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:03.890
- How much pressure
was on him in New York?

00:16:03.890 --> 00:16:07.330
Because he came from 'Bama,
he also had good hair,

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:10.170
his was blonde, was
there a lot of pressure

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:12.370
and expectation on him to live up?

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:14.330
- I would think more than I could realize-

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:16.510
- [Gary] Did you talk to him about it?

00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:18.920
- Not about that, no,
- That's how we did.

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:20.914
Hey, no, man, we were buddies,

00:16:20.914 --> 00:16:21.802
- Talked about going out
- We were like

00:16:21.802 --> 00:16:23.180
- And hanging, yeah.
- New Richard, yeah.

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:25.615
We went to dinner, we hung out,

00:16:25.615 --> 00:16:28.050
we were together, man.

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:31.290
And no, I didn't ask
him about the pressure,

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:33.107
didn't wanna make anything about it, yeah,

00:16:33.107 --> 00:16:34.620
"You gonna follow in my footsteps?"

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:35.453
- None of that.
- No, man,

00:16:35.453 --> 00:16:37.070
it wasn't that kind of thing.

00:16:37.070 --> 00:16:40.730
- Joe, I have another
question, back to nerddom,

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:43.460
what's the biggest thing you learned

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:46.016
from when you went to L.A.?

00:16:46.016 --> 00:16:48.266
(stammers)

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:50.090
At least from what I can see,

00:16:50.090 --> 00:16:53.186
it wasn't a great success with the Rams-

00:16:53.186 --> 00:16:54.596
- No.
- But forget about

00:16:54.596 --> 00:16:56.740
what happened on the
football field for a sec,

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:58.180
or maybe something you
learned on the football field,

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:01.390
I don't know, if I ask you what
was the single biggest thing

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:03.030
you learned

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:05.300
with the Rams experience,

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:06.430
what was it?

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:09.810
- To get something positive
out of a negative season,

00:17:09.810 --> 00:17:13.280
which happened to be the
teammates that I did play with,

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:15.540
Jack Youngblood, Tom Mack, you know,

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:19.300
these kind of cats, Lawrence
McCutcheon, Jackson,

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:21.820
the friendships that I
developed with the players,

00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:24.530
the coach, Chuck Knox there at the time,

00:17:24.530 --> 00:17:27.380
Chuck was our offensive
line coach with the Jets

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:29.117
my rookie season and second season.

00:17:29.117 --> 00:17:30.040
- [Gary] So you had a relationship there.

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:31.830
- Yeah, but

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:33.290
I couldn't play as well,

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:35.980
I had two severed
hamstrings in my left leg

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:39.780
that I played my last five
years or so years with,

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:41.940
and you can't run with
a severed hamstring,

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:44.583
but you can still play
quarterback to some extent.

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:48.590
- When you watch the watch
the current state of the rules

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:50.810
for quarterbacks in the NFL,

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:52.313
do you once in a while,

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:56.730
it's kind of the classic thing
that everybody does, right,

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:57.780
to like a son or a daughter,

00:17:57.780 --> 00:17:59.510
like "In my day, I walked-",

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:02.780
there has to be, when these
phantom 15-yard penalties,

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:04.880
where the guy sneezes on the quarterback,

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:08.100
meanwhile the Raiders are
literally double piledriving you

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:10.560
to the concrete in these games,

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:13.350
at some level, once in
a blue moon on a Sunday

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:14.600
in the last five years,

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:16.787
you've gotta be like
"Geez, what I could've done

00:18:16.787 --> 00:18:19.360
"if this was the rules when I played."

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:20.400
- No.
- Never.

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:21.940
- No, Gary, I tell you what-

00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:23.250
- You don't allow yourself to do that.

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:25.590
- Well, I don't, I was
trained that way, in a sense.

00:18:25.590 --> 00:18:27.140
Coulda, woulda, shoulda-

00:18:27.140 --> 00:18:28.430
- Doesn't matter.
- Excuse me,

00:18:28.430 --> 00:18:29.650
and no "Yeah, but"'s, too. (laughs)

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:30.990
- So forget about you as a human,

00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:32.543
do you just laugh about-

00:18:33.567 --> 00:18:35.064
- [Joe] No, I'm gonna tell you something.

00:18:35.064 --> 00:18:35.897
- Please, I'm listening.

00:18:35.897 --> 00:18:37.050
- The first year I did color,

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:39.530
I did with NBC, I made the statement

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:43.002
I said "I don't care if
Hulk Hogan is back there

00:18:43.002 --> 00:18:43.997
(Gary laughs)
"in the pocket,

00:18:43.997 --> 00:18:45.720
"he's gonna get hurt."

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:48.170
They needed to change the rules.

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:49.003
- I got it.
- They needed,

00:18:49.003 --> 00:18:51.560
because if you knock the best guy out,

00:18:51.560 --> 00:18:54.040
the quality of the game is going downhill,

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:57.130
and the fans are losing out.

00:18:57.130 --> 00:18:59.780
They're not getting the
best possible performance.

00:18:59.780 --> 00:19:02.520
You had to protect the quarterbacks some,

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:05.540
because hey, the backups are backups-

00:19:05.540 --> 00:19:06.640
- [Both] For a reason,

00:19:07.579 --> 00:19:09.040
- Yeah, and you keep going down, man,

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:11.143
it's the quality of game that suffers.

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:16.730
- St. Louis drafted you,
along with the Jets,

00:19:16.730 --> 00:19:20.246
two separate leagues, how
close were you not a Jet?

00:19:20.246 --> 00:19:22.320
Like how much of a shot did
St. Louis actually have?

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:24.670
- You know what-
- For real, for real.

00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:26.295
- For real, for real,
- I'm like, this is one

00:19:26.295 --> 00:19:27.300
- I promise you.
- That I really wanna know.

00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:30.730
- This is all for real,
I promise you that.

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:33.540
The St. Louis Cardinals
showed up unannounced

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:37.770
in Tuscaloosa, Alabama,
in my dorm's lobby.

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:39.510
I'm up in my room-

00:19:39.510 --> 00:19:42.320
- Doing what? (laughs)

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:45.814
- It's daytime.
- With you, bro, I don't know.

00:19:45.814 --> 00:19:47.214
(laughs)
I'm up in my room,

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:50.467
a knock, "Hey, Namith,
there's a couple of guys

00:19:50.467 --> 00:19:51.330
"to see you."

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:53.300
I didn't even know who drafted me,

00:19:53.300 --> 00:19:54.724
it wasn't like today-

00:19:54.724 --> 00:19:56.060
- I know, I know.
- We didn't know,

00:19:56.060 --> 00:19:58.970
it wasn't televised,
it wasn't phone calls,

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:01.710
you know, I mean, these
two guys came upstairs,

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:04.823
one was the general manager
and one was one of the owners,

00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:07.430
and they sat down on my bed

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:08.470
'cause I wouldn't let them sit

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:09.963
on my roommate's bed over there,

00:20:09.963 --> 00:20:10.796
(Gary laughs)

00:20:10.796 --> 00:20:12.750
my room with Pelham, Butch Henry,

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:15.371
was named after General Pelham,

00:20:15.371 --> 00:20:17.570
the southerner, you know, the Confederacy,

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:19.573
anyway, we sat on the bunks,

00:20:21.282 --> 00:20:24.740
and the owner guy told
me they had drafted me,

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:27.883
and wanted to sign me to a contract,

00:20:29.294 --> 00:20:30.787
and I said "Oh well, good,

00:20:30.787 --> 00:20:32.880
"but I haven't heard
from the other team yet,"

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:34.057
he said "Well that's all right,

00:20:34.057 --> 00:20:36.457
"you're not gonna have to listen to them,

00:20:36.457 --> 00:20:37.887
"we're gonna sign you,

00:20:37.887 --> 00:20:39.520
"and we're gonna offer you-"

00:20:39.520 --> 00:20:41.920
Well, no, it wasn't
"We're gonna offer you",

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:44.550
they asked me what I wanted to play,

00:20:44.550 --> 00:20:47.890
and Coach Bryant had asked
me that same question

00:20:47.890 --> 00:20:51.490
after the Auburn game, our last
game of the regular season,

00:20:51.490 --> 00:20:54.150
and I told Coach Bryant
in the locker room,

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:56.810
as I was down on my knee putting the sock

00:20:56.810 --> 00:20:59.590
on our backup quarterback
Steve Sloan's leg,

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:01.320
'cause he was hurt, too,

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:02.797
and I said "Well, coach,

00:21:02.797 --> 00:21:06.410
"Don Trull signed for 100,000 last year,"

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:08.700
I said "I was thinking of 100,000,"

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:10.230
looking up at Coach Bryant.

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:11.740
Coach Bryant took a draw

00:21:11.740 --> 00:21:14.930
off that big ol' Chesterfield
cigarette he was doing there,

00:21:14.930 --> 00:21:17.190
and he (blows) blew some smoke,

00:21:17.190 --> 00:21:18.153
and he said "Well,

00:21:19.637 --> 00:21:22.367
"you go ahead and ask them for 200,000,"

00:21:22.367 --> 00:21:25.021
he said, "That's the
better place to start."

00:21:25.021 --> 00:21:26.120
(laughs)
- That's amazing.

00:21:26.120 --> 00:21:28.037
- So these guys, he said

00:21:28.037 --> 00:21:30.300
"Well, what do you think
you're gonna want here?",

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:32.887
I told him, I could hardly
get it out, you know-

00:21:32.887 --> 00:21:36.030
- 200,000.
- 200,00 dollars.

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:38.457
And the guy fell back,

00:21:38.457 --> 00:21:40.270
"Oh, 200,000!"

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:43.630
The other guy starts,
"200,000, you crazy?"

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:45.023
and I remembered,

00:21:45.980 --> 00:21:48.217
"Oh, and the new car, too."
(Gary laughs)

00:21:48.217 --> 00:21:50.767
"Oh, you want a new car too, do you?

00:21:50.767 --> 00:21:51.850
"You want a new car?"

00:21:51.850 --> 00:21:53.270
And I said "Well, yes, sir."

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:55.109
- You skipped baseball,

00:21:55.109 --> 00:21:57.467
you gotta get that baseball
car back, I get it. (laughs)

00:21:57.467 --> 00:21:58.390
- He asked me what kind of car,

00:21:58.390 --> 00:22:01.230
'cause I talked an old
high school team roommate

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:02.780
maybe a few days before,

00:22:02.780 --> 00:22:05.670
and he asked me what I was gonna ask for,

00:22:05.670 --> 00:22:07.570
and I told him about Coach Bryant,

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:09.040
and asking me that money,

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:11.850
and he said, "Well man, what
kind of Short you gonna get?"

00:22:11.850 --> 00:22:14.330
I said "Well, I don't know,
I didn't think about that."

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:15.787
He said "Oh, you need to get you a

00:22:15.787 --> 00:22:18.594
"Lincoln Continental convertible."

00:22:18.594 --> 00:22:19.427
(Gary laughs)
He said

00:22:19.427 --> 00:22:20.347
"It's got them barn doors,

00:22:20.347 --> 00:22:22.670
"those doors that open
out this way," you know,

00:22:22.670 --> 00:22:23.580
and so

00:22:24.722 --> 00:22:25.555
I told

00:22:26.582 --> 00:22:27.810
the owner and the general manager,

00:22:27.810 --> 00:22:30.690
I said "A Lincoln Continental."

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:31.784
They looked at each other and said,

00:22:31.784 --> 00:22:32.947
(mockingly) "Oh, sure!

00:22:32.947 --> 00:22:35.090
"He wants a Lincoln Continental!"

00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:37.569
Made me feel like a real jerk.

00:22:37.569 --> 00:22:38.610
- And so how did it end?

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:40.340
Did they say "Thank
you" and kind of leave?

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:41.900
- No, it didn't end there,

00:22:41.900 --> 00:22:44.903
the man went in his briefcase
and pulled out a contract.

00:22:46.290 --> 00:22:47.930
Pulled out a contract.

00:22:47.930 --> 00:22:50.290
And he said "Now look, okay, all right,"

00:22:50.290 --> 00:22:52.957
and he's got this contract,
he's gonna pass it,

00:22:52.957 --> 00:22:54.867
"No, no, no, no, wait a minute.

00:22:54.867 --> 00:22:57.120
"We got a ball game yet."

00:22:57.120 --> 00:23:00.329
Coach Bryant had told myself
and the other guys, certainly,

00:23:00.329 --> 00:23:01.162
- Can't sign it
- That were seniors.

00:23:01.162 --> 00:23:02.117
- [Gary] Until your course.

00:23:02.117 --> 00:23:04.900
- Yeah, we had a ball game
coming up, January first!

00:23:04.900 --> 00:23:08.022
So they tried to get
me to sign right then.

00:23:08.022 --> 00:23:09.450
- Of course.
- They knew the rules.

00:23:09.450 --> 00:23:11.840
But, you know, that's okay,
I just had to tell them no,

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:14.860
I can't do that now, we've
got a game coming up.

00:23:14.860 --> 00:23:17.060
Coach Bryant actually told us

00:23:17.060 --> 00:23:18.357
that if we were being recruited,

00:23:18.357 --> 00:23:21.057
"Don't you even take a Coca-Cola from 'em.

00:23:21.057 --> 00:23:23.010
"Any of these guys,", that's right,

00:23:23.010 --> 00:23:24.560
'cause we had a game coming up.

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:27.010
- So you got them out of the room somehow

00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:27.843
without signing the deal.

00:23:27.843 --> 00:23:28.950
- Yes, that's right.

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:31.470
And I sat there alone,

00:23:31.470 --> 00:23:33.327
wondering "Wow,

00:23:33.327 --> 00:23:35.480
"what do I do now?"

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:36.763
So I called a buddy,

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:40.700
a student, a regular student,

00:23:40.700 --> 00:23:42.449
he was a dealer in Las Vegas

00:23:42.449 --> 00:23:44.020
(Gary laughs)
in between semesters,

00:23:44.020 --> 00:23:45.840
Hugh B., and he came over, picked me up,

00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:48.290
we drove down to a restaurant
called The Deep South

00:23:48.290 --> 00:23:50.620
for a cup of coffee to talk this over.

00:23:50.620 --> 00:23:51.453
- That's cool.
- And as he and I

00:23:51.453 --> 00:23:55.610
are talking, I said "Damn,
I gotta get some help."

00:23:55.610 --> 00:23:57.490
So I got up and went to the pay phone,

00:23:57.490 --> 00:23:59.520
at the time it was a
dime for the pay phone,

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:01.960
and I called a buddy up in
Alabama who was a lawyer,

00:24:01.960 --> 00:24:06.020
Mr. Michael "Hike'll" Bite,
and told him the situation,

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:09.260
and so then I had legal representation,

00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:11.580
and Mike handed it the rest of the way

00:24:11.580 --> 00:24:13.995
for that initial contract.

00:24:13.995 --> 00:24:14.860
- [Gary] And the Jets won.

00:24:14.860 --> 00:24:18.850
- Well, the Jets won, they
flew me and Mike, actually,

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:22.250
Mike paid for the plane
fare to fly to Los Angeles,

00:24:22.250 --> 00:24:25.490
and I met Mr. Werblin in Beverly Hills,

00:24:25.490 --> 00:24:28.187
at the Beverly Hills
Hotel, trees and sunny.

00:24:28.187 --> 00:24:30.674
- That was a smart move by Sonny, uh-huh.

00:24:30.674 --> 00:24:32.483
- Unbelievable.

00:24:33.492 --> 00:24:35.470
And it was spectacular.

00:24:35.470 --> 00:24:38.190
And he wanted to sign with the Jets,

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:39.860
and we were able to go to a game

00:24:39.860 --> 00:24:42.290
and watch the Jets and
the San Diego chargers

00:24:42.290 --> 00:24:44.880
play down in San Diego.

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:48.020
We drove down there, and
then I went back to Alabama,

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:51.430
and we met again with Mr. Werblin,

00:24:51.430 --> 00:24:54.319
well, he was the man
doing everything then.

00:24:54.319 --> 00:24:55.162
- Yeah, he was.

00:24:55.162 --> 00:24:56.810
There was a big partnership,
but he was running it.

00:24:56.810 --> 00:24:58.730
- Yeah, yes, that's right,

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:01.970
and had Weeb Eubank
being the general manager

00:25:01.970 --> 00:25:03.950
who knew something about
football, you know,

00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:07.040
the other owners were in
thoroughbred industry,

00:25:07.040 --> 00:25:10.100
the oil industry, you
know, the fashion industry,

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:12.194
they were different, and

00:25:12.194 --> 00:25:14.670
it's just the kind of guy Mr. Werblin was,

00:25:14.670 --> 00:25:16.940
was an influence, and that's the thing,

00:25:16.940 --> 00:25:19.637
Coach Bryant told me
"Get to know the people

00:25:19.637 --> 00:25:21.677
"you're gonna work for."

00:25:22.540 --> 00:25:23.373
- [Gary] 100 percent.

00:25:23.373 --> 00:25:26.963
- And that was so important
because it became a no contest,

00:25:27.810 --> 00:25:29.020
after the two guys.

00:25:29.020 --> 00:25:31.370
- How quickly did your
relationship with Sunny

00:25:31.370 --> 00:25:32.773
turn into him saying,

00:25:34.050 --> 00:25:36.767
did he communicate to you that "Hey, kid,

00:25:36.767 --> 00:25:38.790
"I'm gonna make you a superstar?"

00:25:38.790 --> 00:25:41.860
His blueprint on treating you

00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:44.810
like he treated other
celebrities from L.A.

00:25:44.810 --> 00:25:48.540
was not a model that was in
sports up until that point.

00:25:48.540 --> 00:25:52.070
I mean that Broadway Joe thing
is real, real, real stuff.

00:25:52.070 --> 00:25:54.340
I don't think people
understand in this day and age

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:57.120
what Sports Illustrated
meant, what that cover meant,

00:25:57.120 --> 00:25:59.800
every single young guy in the country

00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:02.230
sees that magazine cover,

00:26:02.230 --> 00:26:04.093
and that positions you right away.

00:26:05.310 --> 00:26:07.440
Did you know that that was going on,

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:09.000
or was he just kind of doing it,

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:10.890
or was that something you were a part of.

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:12.400
- Gary, you're right on.

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:13.560
You're right on.

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:15.130
It was Mr. Werblin.

00:26:15.130 --> 00:26:19.160
At one point, I overheard
him telling someone else,

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:22.070
whether it was he and
his wife and I, together,

00:26:22.070 --> 00:26:23.117
or whoever,

00:26:23.117 --> 00:26:25.910
"Stars sell tickets."

00:26:25.910 --> 00:26:27.850
He created the star system,

00:26:27.850 --> 00:26:29.130
- He sure did.
- So to speak,

00:26:29.130 --> 00:26:30.603
in professional football.

00:26:30.603 --> 00:26:32.580
- He did.
- We had Red Grange

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:35.540
many years ago, Jim Brown
was a star, you know,

00:26:35.540 --> 00:26:37.010
- [Gary] There were
Yankees that were stars.

00:26:37.010 --> 00:26:39.250
- Catered to in a sense,
they weren't built up,

00:26:39.250 --> 00:26:40.467
we weren't built up.

00:26:40.467 --> 00:26:41.960
- And you crossed over.

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:45.210
- He embraced the media in New York.

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:48.670
He started inviting the media into areas

00:26:48.670 --> 00:26:51.100
and buying them lunch
and visiting with them.

00:26:51.100 --> 00:26:53.653
He reached out to the media,

00:26:55.375 --> 00:26:57.470
and he got it off the ground, man,

00:26:57.470 --> 00:26:59.310
I mean it wouldn't surprised me

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:01.470
if he called Sports Illustrated himself

00:27:01.470 --> 00:27:03.670
to get that picture on Broadway.

00:27:03.670 --> 00:27:07.170
Yeah, he was instrumental
not only in my career,

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:09.886
but in the AFL's existence.

00:27:09.886 --> 00:27:11.620
- How did it feel the
first time you saw yourself

00:27:11.620 --> 00:27:13.560
on the cover of Sports
Illustrated like that?

00:27:13.560 --> 00:27:16.950
- I was stunned.
(Gary laughs)

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:20.780
First time I saw it, we
were practicing at Shea,

00:27:20.780 --> 00:27:24.790
I came in off the practice
field, and on my stool,

00:27:24.790 --> 00:27:27.720
and every other player's
stool around the locker room,

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:30.870
was that magazine, the Sports Illustrated,

00:27:30.870 --> 00:27:33.660
with my smiling face looking up.

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:36.660
I had a few veterans pick
up the magazine, look at it,

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:38.240
and then throw it at me, you know,

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:41.020
I'm ducking magazines,
you know, embarrassed,

00:27:41.020 --> 00:27:41.853
actually,

00:27:43.840 --> 00:27:46.290
it was kind of scary,

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:48.693
some of them were cursing
a little bit about it,

00:27:49.789 --> 00:27:51.200
and I'm sitting here straight across,

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:52.480
like where you're sitting over there,

00:27:52.480 --> 00:27:53.500
straight across from me,

00:27:53.500 --> 00:27:55.920
the offensive linemen are sitting there,

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:58.520
and our offensive
leader, Sherman Plunkett,

00:27:58.520 --> 00:28:01.800
offensive tackle, who was about 6'5",

00:28:01.800 --> 00:28:03.635
and we didn't know how much he weighed,

00:28:03.635 --> 00:28:04.468
(Gary laughs)

00:28:04.468 --> 00:28:08.350
because the scales only went
up to 300 then, you see.

00:28:08.350 --> 00:28:10.470
And I used to carry Sherman's helmet

00:28:10.470 --> 00:28:14.140
off the practice field, from
training camp to that day,

00:28:14.140 --> 00:28:15.410
he would leave his helmet there

00:28:15.410 --> 00:28:18.090
and I would carry it off for him, right,

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:20.200
and I'm sitting there and
I look across over there

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:23.133
and there's Sherman,
looking at the magazine.

00:28:23.133 --> 00:28:24.650
(Gary laughs)
And I'm looking at Sherman,

00:28:24.650 --> 00:28:26.310
and then Sherman picks his head up,

00:28:26.310 --> 00:28:27.840
and he looks over at me,

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:31.013
he smiles, and just the
words came out his mouth,

00:28:31.957 --> 00:28:33.887
"Ol' Broadway!

00:28:33.887 --> 00:28:35.150
"Broadway Joe!"

00:28:35.150 --> 00:28:38.590
And then from that time on,
it was cool, it was nice.

00:28:38.590 --> 00:28:41.247
- He cosigned it, he
allowed the rest of the team

00:28:41.247 --> 00:28:43.160
- Well, for him to say that,
- To embrace it.

00:28:43.160 --> 00:28:46.350
- Yeah, that caught on,
that was cool, yeah.

00:28:46.350 --> 00:28:47.610
- Really interesting.

00:28:47.610 --> 00:28:49.700
It's super interesting
for me to look from afar

00:28:49.700 --> 00:28:52.690
at somebody who thinks about
attention and marketing,

00:28:52.690 --> 00:28:55.680
that whole process, jumping to:

00:28:55.680 --> 00:28:58.140
Did you have any idea that
the pantyhose commercial

00:28:58.140 --> 00:28:59.717
was going to be that big?

00:28:59.717 --> 00:29:02.490
- You know what, this is the guy over here

00:29:02.490 --> 00:29:06.310
that handled that
situation, Mr Walsh and I-

00:29:06.310 --> 00:29:07.180
- [Gary] Tell me about that.

00:29:07.180 --> 00:29:09.940
- We were together in Tuscaloosa, Alabama,

00:29:09.940 --> 00:29:11.850
New Jersey guy,

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:12.940
Hungarian-Irish,

00:29:12.940 --> 00:29:15.306
here's Hungarian-Pennsylvanian, you know-

00:29:15.306 --> 00:29:16.139
- East Coast, yeah.
- We're in Tuscaloosa,

00:29:16.139 --> 00:29:18.470
Alabama, that's where
our friendship started,

00:29:18.470 --> 00:29:22.430
when Jimmy came to New York and
passed the bar exam up here,

00:29:22.430 --> 00:29:26.103
he's an attorney, we
started working together.

00:29:26.103 --> 00:29:27.657
And Jimmy called and says "Joe,

00:29:27.657 --> 00:29:29.607
"I have an interesting proposal here,

00:29:29.607 --> 00:29:30.810
"I've got a storyboard-"

00:29:30.810 --> 00:29:32.388
- And Jimmy, real quick,
and I apologize, Joe,

00:29:32.388 --> 00:29:35.270
at that point, were you getting
tons of different things,

00:29:35.270 --> 00:29:36.480
and you were vetting them?

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:37.313
- [Jimmy] Yes.

00:29:37.313 --> 00:29:38.840
- And this one just caught your eye?

00:29:38.840 --> 00:29:41.587
Did they, when they pitched it to you, say

00:29:41.587 --> 00:29:43.710
"Look, Joe's legs are banged up,"

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:46.720
was it very easy to see
the cleverness of it,

00:29:46.720 --> 00:29:48.841
or did that develop later as an idea?

00:29:48.841 --> 00:29:50.130
- [Jimmy] No, there was a
lady from Photoplay Magazine,

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:52.360
who went to the University of Alabama,

00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:53.930
and she was the editor of Photoplay,

00:29:53.930 --> 00:29:55.310
and she happened to come in the office

00:29:55.310 --> 00:29:56.939
to talk to me about this,

00:29:56.939 --> 00:29:58.740
talked to me about doing a thing for her,

00:29:58.740 --> 00:30:01.027
and basically at that point,

00:30:01.027 --> 00:30:02.037
and I asked her, I said

00:30:02.037 --> 00:30:04.110
"Well, what do you think about this idea?"

00:30:04.110 --> 00:30:06.267
and she said "My father is an Iowan,

00:30:06.267 --> 00:30:09.430
"he would be devastated if Joe
Namath ever wore pantyhose."

00:30:09.430 --> 00:30:11.447
(Jimmy talks under Gary)
- Yeah, honestly,

00:30:11.447 --> 00:30:13.290
Joe wearing panty, what year was it?

00:30:13.290 --> 00:30:14.343
70, what?

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:16.326
Do you remember?

00:30:16.326 --> 00:30:17.488
'75?

00:30:17.488 --> 00:30:18.442
- [Jimmy] '72, maybe.

00:30:18.442 --> 00:30:19.640
- Okay.

00:30:19.640 --> 00:30:21.850
That is a risky move.

00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:24.000
- You know what, no, man, I liked it,

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:25.137
I saw the humor in it,

00:30:25.137 --> 00:30:27.720
and first of all, it was a
job, and it was a good job,

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:29.621
and it was with a good company.

00:30:29.621 --> 00:30:31.643
- By the way, I've seen the
commercial a billion times,

00:30:31.643 --> 00:30:33.777
were you blown away how good
your legs actually looked,

00:30:33.777 --> 00:30:36.010
you know, the commercial, for
anybody who's not watching,

00:30:36.010 --> 00:30:39.640
maybe we'll edit it on the
vlog, it's a pan-up of legs,

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:41.990
and it's coming kind of up from the toes,

00:30:41.990 --> 00:30:45.620
from left to right, and
it's nice-looking legs,

00:30:45.620 --> 00:30:47.684
and then all of a sudden, it's Joe!

00:30:47.684 --> 00:30:49.601
(crew laughs)

00:30:49.601 --> 00:30:51.380
Were you even stunned
when you first saw it?

00:30:51.380 --> 00:30:52.870
Like "Man, these things really work"?

00:30:52.870 --> 00:30:54.115
- Wait a minute.

00:30:54.115 --> 00:30:55.630
(Gary laughs)

00:30:55.630 --> 00:30:59.892
If Beautymist can make
these legs look beautiful,

00:30:59.892 --> 00:31:00.725
(crew laughs)

00:31:00.725 --> 00:31:01.558
- Still got it.
- Just think

00:31:01.558 --> 00:31:03.070
what they can do for yours.

00:31:03.070 --> 00:31:04.127
- Real good.
(laughs)

00:31:04.127 --> 00:31:05.418
- That's amazing.
- Beautymist by Hanes.

00:31:05.418 --> 00:31:06.376
- Was that one take?

00:31:06.376 --> 00:31:08.207
- No, it wasn't one take.

00:31:08.207 --> 00:31:09.168
- [Jimmy] That wasn't the first one,

00:31:09.168 --> 00:31:11.210
Joe did a commercial for
pantyhose before that

00:31:11.210 --> 00:31:13.420
for a company called Alba-Waldensian,

00:31:13.420 --> 00:31:14.610
but that was just a print ad,

00:31:14.610 --> 00:31:17.093
nobody saw that until a year or two ago.

00:31:17.093 --> 00:31:20.243
- I don't remember that,
by the way. (laughs)

00:31:20.243 --> 00:31:22.970
- Let's talk about commentating,
you make that transition.

00:31:22.970 --> 00:31:24.600
You did that for a while.

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:26.204
- By the way, wait a minute,

00:31:26.204 --> 00:31:27.037
I'm gonna interrupt you,

00:31:27.037 --> 00:31:28.030
- Please, go ahead.
- Gary, now,

00:31:28.030 --> 00:31:30.380
you know, you play
soccer, you play football,

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:33.210
even sometimes basketball,
and you tape your ankles

00:31:33.210 --> 00:31:35.400
and knees, you have to shave your legs.

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:36.630
- Makes sense.
- So my legs

00:31:36.630 --> 00:31:38.170
were already shaved, you see.

00:31:38.170 --> 00:31:39.470
- Super comfortable.
- I didn't even

00:31:39.470 --> 00:31:40.703
have to shave my legs.

00:31:40.703 --> 00:31:42.320
(both laugh)

00:31:42.320 --> 00:31:44.420
They was clean, they was smooth. (laughs)

00:31:44.420 --> 00:31:46.440
- Talk to me about
sportscasting, you enjoyed it?

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:47.629
Was that fun?

00:31:47.629 --> 00:31:48.690
Or was it

00:31:49.840 --> 00:31:52.670
a nice thing to do, where did that sit?

00:31:52.670 --> 00:31:56.740
- I wasn't educated enough
to know that the controversy,

00:31:56.740 --> 00:31:58.980
the critiquing needed to be harsher,

00:31:58.980 --> 00:32:01.770
I was more of a protective player,

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:05.796
still being a football
locker room kind of teammate,

00:32:05.796 --> 00:32:08.550
and I wasn't really keen on it.

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:09.383
- I understand.

00:32:09.383 --> 00:32:10.790
Talk to me about, you know,

00:32:10.790 --> 00:32:12.290
I think a lot of people,
when they think about,

00:32:12.290 --> 00:32:14.310
back to nerding out a little bit,

00:32:14.310 --> 00:32:17.170
a lot of people talk about Don Maynard.

00:32:17.170 --> 00:32:18.730
I want to talk about George Sauer.

00:32:18.730 --> 00:32:20.383
What kind of teammate was he?

00:32:22.600 --> 00:32:24.120
Just talk to me about George.

00:32:24.120 --> 00:32:27.410
- Well, they're both
from Texas, as you know,

00:32:27.410 --> 00:32:29.140
and you mentioned Don Maynard first,

00:32:29.140 --> 00:32:31.610
and I want to go back to
that Kansas City Chiefs game,

00:32:31.610 --> 00:32:34.490
we didn't have Don in
that game, he was hurt.

00:32:34.490 --> 00:32:36.240
We had another Texan, Bake Turner,

00:32:36.240 --> 00:32:38.920
but they also had a quarterback
that was really good,

00:32:38.920 --> 00:32:40.310
he was in the Hall of Fame,

00:32:40.310 --> 00:32:43.060
and so they shut down our
game pretty good that day.

00:32:43.060 --> 00:32:43.973
George Sauer,

00:32:46.070 --> 00:32:49.420
I have often said that Don
Maynard is in the Hall of Fame,

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:52.060
he was the lighting
strike, the knockout punch,

00:32:52.060 --> 00:32:55.530
every team had to respect
his tricky speed, man.

00:32:55.530 --> 00:32:57.617
He was wily,

00:32:57.617 --> 00:32:58.893
he was terrific.

00:33:00.040 --> 00:33:03.160
The guy I liked working
best with was George Sauer.

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:05.506
George Sauer and I were rookies together-

00:33:05.506 --> 00:33:07.540
- Was he great at route running?

00:33:07.540 --> 00:33:09.560
- He was wonderful at route running.

00:33:09.560 --> 00:33:10.393
- You knew where he was gonna be.

00:33:10.393 --> 00:33:11.520
- Yes, yes.

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:13.930
We would work after practice, man,

00:33:13.930 --> 00:33:18.810
and as rookies together,
and we developed the timing,

00:33:18.810 --> 00:33:20.510
it was just wonderful.

00:33:20.510 --> 00:33:24.130
And some of the guys, the
veterans would talk about,

00:33:24.130 --> 00:33:28.290
even the coaches, Don's lack
of discipline in route running.

00:33:28.290 --> 00:33:30.580
Well, excuse me, he knew how to get open.

00:33:30.580 --> 00:33:32.160
He might've rounded some routes

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:33.420
- Of course, of course.
- Off a little differently

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:35.700
than breaking them sharply or whatever,

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:37.152
he's a Hall-of-Famer,

00:33:37.152 --> 00:33:38.190
- He sure was.
- He was a man.

00:33:38.190 --> 00:33:42.420
George Sauer, you could
always count on George.

00:33:42.420 --> 00:33:44.930
I mean, being there precise, man.

00:33:44.930 --> 00:33:47.430
And he had hands that,

00:33:47.430 --> 00:33:49.280
I don't remember him dropping anything.

00:33:49.280 --> 00:33:52.430
In the Super Bowl, in
the championship game,

00:33:52.430 --> 00:33:55.520
there was a play that
George caught a quick out,

00:33:55.520 --> 00:33:57.830
got hit, and fumbled the ball.

00:33:57.830 --> 00:33:59.610
Today they wouldn't call it a fumble,

00:33:59.610 --> 00:34:02.410
they'd call it incomplete
pass, it was that fast,

00:34:02.410 --> 00:34:03.670
Lenny Lyles hit him.

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:05.389
George was wonderful.

00:34:05.389 --> 00:34:07.963
- Joe, what about now, fandom-wise,

00:34:09.215 --> 00:34:11.450
how much do you watch 'Bama or the Jets,

00:34:11.450 --> 00:34:13.240
or anybody else, for that matter?

00:34:13.240 --> 00:34:16.207
- I watch 'Bama and I watch
the Jets weekly (laughs),

00:34:17.776 --> 00:34:18.609
- I love it.
- And in this day and age-

00:34:18.609 --> 00:34:21.083
- And you have since you've retired?

00:34:22.415 --> 00:34:24.460
Or you took some time off, or how did your

00:34:24.460 --> 00:34:26.500
fandom post-career develop?

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:28.440
Did you take some time
off after you retired

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:31.110
'cause you just needed to
in that point of your life,

00:34:31.110 --> 00:34:33.460
or has literally from the
day you stopped playing,

00:34:33.460 --> 00:34:34.680
Saturday and Sunday you watch?

00:34:34.680 --> 00:34:36.982
- I did take some time away from football,

00:34:36.982 --> 00:34:38.000
- Makes sense.
- I'm a Gemini,

00:34:38.000 --> 00:34:39.240
(Gary laughs)
there's a side of me, really,

00:34:39.240 --> 00:34:40.520
- I get it.
- And I believe

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:41.480
in this kind of thing, really.

00:34:41.480 --> 00:34:43.750
I wanted out, I wanted a change of pace,

00:34:43.750 --> 00:34:45.360
- Makes a ton of sense.
- I wanna experiment,

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:47.830
I wanted to learn from
these other opportunities

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:48.990
that I did have.

00:34:48.990 --> 00:34:51.232
- Acting, that was
- Yes, exactly.

00:34:51.232 --> 00:34:52.065
- A big focus of yours.
- I went and

00:34:52.065 --> 00:34:55.080
took some lessons, some
acting, I studied acting,

00:34:55.080 --> 00:34:57.630
I studied voice, and I
did some other things

00:34:58.470 --> 00:35:01.090
with theater, as well as motion pictures,

00:35:01.090 --> 00:35:03.510
but never had the real passion to do it.

00:35:03.510 --> 00:35:06.090
They were nice jobs, they were good jobs,

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:08.160
but there's a side of this Gemini

00:35:09.159 --> 00:35:11.350
that I need downtime.

00:35:11.350 --> 00:35:13.053
It's for your health, you know,

00:35:14.270 --> 00:35:15.103
I won't

00:35:16.677 --> 00:35:17.606
work that way,

00:35:17.606 --> 00:35:18.470
- I get it.
- Spending hours,

00:35:18.470 --> 00:35:21.470
and then life evolved
into having a family,

00:35:21.470 --> 00:35:23.790
and I enjoy spending time with the family

00:35:23.790 --> 00:35:25.580
and also that was cool.

00:35:25.580 --> 00:35:28.430
Leaving football, we've
talked about coaching,

00:35:28.430 --> 00:35:30.240
those coaches work so hard.

00:35:30.240 --> 00:35:31.690
- So hard.
- You gotta have

00:35:31.690 --> 00:35:33.090
the passion to do it.

00:35:33.090 --> 00:35:35.717
The passion, what you're
doing, how you go,

00:35:35.717 --> 00:35:37.280
- 100 percent.
- You got that passion, man,

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:38.513
you know, that's right.

00:35:39.650 --> 00:35:41.440
So when I got out of football,

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:43.970
I wanted a break, I wanted to get away,

00:35:43.970 --> 00:35:47.390
and Jimmy continued to
guide me in directions

00:35:47.390 --> 00:35:49.120
that we kept growing.

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:51.200
- And then you kind of got the bug back

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:52.740
a little bit again to start just watching

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:54.030
on Saturdays and Sundays?

00:35:54.030 --> 00:35:57.750
- No, you know what, NFL
Ticket came into play.

00:35:57.750 --> 00:36:01.150
You could watch your pro team,
every game, every play, man.

00:36:01.150 --> 00:36:02.520
- You were in Florida at the time,

00:36:02.520 --> 00:36:03.670
I'm trying to remember.

00:36:03.670 --> 00:36:06.730
- I was in Florida at the time, I think-

00:36:06.730 --> 00:36:07.850
- So you were able to
start watching again.

00:36:07.850 --> 00:36:12.360
- We started out with
Classic Sports Network

00:36:12.360 --> 00:36:14.480
was a board that I was on

00:36:14.480 --> 00:36:17.889
with Ernie Banks and Will
Chamberlain and Larry Bird.

00:36:17.889 --> 00:36:19.056
- Brian Bedol.

00:36:20.210 --> 00:36:21.243
- Greenberg,

00:36:22.470 --> 00:36:24.540
Steven Greenberg and Brian Bedol,

00:36:24.540 --> 00:36:27.008
started that, Brian, god bless him.

00:36:27.008 --> 00:36:28.160
- Great dude, spoke to him last week.

00:36:28.160 --> 00:36:31.813
- And Classic Sports
Network was wonderful-

00:36:32.881 --> 00:36:33.714
- It was wonderful, you know what?

00:36:33.714 --> 00:36:35.030
Classic Sports Network was wonderful.

00:36:35.030 --> 00:36:37.920
It allowed a generation, my generation,

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:40.850
to really get a sense of
some of the icons of sports.

00:36:40.850 --> 00:36:43.700
I mean, it was a really
thoughtful product.

00:36:43.700 --> 00:36:45.240
I really enjoyed it,

00:36:45.240 --> 00:36:48.180
I couldn't agree more, nostalgia plays.

00:36:48.180 --> 00:36:49.340
Nostalgia plays.

00:36:49.340 --> 00:36:51.730
- Of all sports, you
know, not just football.

00:36:51.730 --> 00:36:52.840
- So as a Jets fan,

00:36:52.840 --> 00:36:54.680
what has been the most heartbreaking loss

00:36:54.680 --> 00:36:56.240
for you as a fan?

00:36:56.240 --> 00:36:57.133
Not as a player.

00:36:58.240 --> 00:36:59.700
- Not my heart breaking.

00:36:59.700 --> 00:37:01.190
I don't get heartbroken

00:37:01.190 --> 00:37:03.880
- You stay even-keeled?
- Over sports, you know?

00:37:03.880 --> 00:37:05.740
- Well, there are more important things-

00:37:05.740 --> 00:37:07.670
- I met you very briefly,

00:37:07.670 --> 00:37:09.580
'cause we were both walking on the field

00:37:09.580 --> 00:37:10.850
for the Pittsburgh game,

00:37:10.850 --> 00:37:13.830
the Doug Bryan game when
he missed two field goals

00:37:13.830 --> 00:37:16.270
within a minute, that was a heartbreaker.

00:37:16.270 --> 00:37:19.670
But I weirdly, if you
remember, Alex, I wasn't upset.

00:37:19.670 --> 00:37:21.120
There's this secret, Joe,

00:37:21.120 --> 00:37:24.220
my ambition in life is
to buy the New York Jets.

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:27.140
That is 100 percent
what I spend my time on,

00:37:27.140 --> 00:37:28.770
and there is a secret part of me

00:37:28.770 --> 00:37:32.210
that wonders if I actually
wanna be the person

00:37:32.210 --> 00:37:33.740
that brings back the championship,

00:37:33.740 --> 00:37:35.860
so I'm a huge fan, I watch every play,

00:37:35.860 --> 00:37:37.500
I watch every game, three years ago,

00:37:37.500 --> 00:37:39.620
my brother and I went to
all 16 games on the road,

00:37:39.620 --> 00:37:41.150
it's a big passion of mine,

00:37:41.150 --> 00:37:44.070
but there was something
weird after the Steelers game

00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:46.630
where I wasn't as upset
as the rest of the crew,

00:37:46.630 --> 00:37:48.490
and we drove back from Pittsburgh,

00:37:48.490 --> 00:37:49.817
and that was the first time I said

00:37:49.817 --> 00:37:52.317
"Do I actually want this
team to win a Super Bowl,

00:37:52.317 --> 00:37:55.052
"or do I wanna buy this
team and win a Super Bowl?"

00:37:55.052 --> 00:37:55.885
(Joe laughs)
Like that was the first time

00:37:55.885 --> 00:37:56.840
I actually asked it.

00:37:56.840 --> 00:37:58.290
- Well, how do you feel now?
- So you don't get crushed.

00:37:58.290 --> 00:37:59.123
- Do you still actually-

00:37:59.123 --> 00:38:00.850
- Listen, I will say this.

00:38:00.850 --> 00:38:02.900
You know, outside of Pennington's

00:38:04.921 --> 00:38:05.754
first 18,

00:38:05.754 --> 00:38:06.940
before he got hurt, that first season

00:38:06.940 --> 00:38:08.540
when he had that incredible run,

00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:11.740
and as much as I was excited about Favre,

00:38:11.740 --> 00:38:12.950
but I knew it was later in his career,

00:38:12.950 --> 00:38:17.260
and that Favre team, late in
that season before he got hurt,

00:38:17.260 --> 00:38:19.970
was probably, when they beat the Titans,

00:38:19.970 --> 00:38:22.380
who were undefeated at
the time in week 11,

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:24.157
that was maybe one of
the few times I was like

00:38:24.157 --> 00:38:25.160
"Maybe this is the team",

00:38:25.160 --> 00:38:28.310
but this Darnold kid, and
I've gotten to know him

00:38:28.310 --> 00:38:30.690
because my brother and I
started this sports agency,

00:38:30.690 --> 00:38:31.700
we recruited him.

00:38:31.700 --> 00:38:34.180
We were in his living room in California,

00:38:34.180 --> 00:38:35.660
trying to convince him
to go with our agency.

00:38:35.660 --> 00:38:38.340
He didn't go with us, but
we struck such a friendship,

00:38:38.340 --> 00:38:40.080
and the last thing I said to his mother

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:42.327
when we were pitching him, I said

00:38:42.327 --> 00:38:45.387
"Mrs. Darnold, if I had the option,

00:38:45.387 --> 00:38:47.017
"if God was listening to me right now,

00:38:47.017 --> 00:38:48.787
"and your son would sign with us

00:38:48.787 --> 00:38:51.047
"and go number one overall to Cleveland,

00:38:51.047 --> 00:38:54.337
"or not go with us and
go three to the Jets,

00:38:54.337 --> 00:38:56.840
"I would pick not go with
us and three to the Jets."

00:38:56.840 --> 00:38:58.790
- Oh man. (laughs)
- So I'm extremely,

00:38:59.898 --> 00:39:04.040
this is no question, it's
a quarterback league,

00:39:04.040 --> 00:39:06.980
and I do think it takes this

00:39:06.980 --> 00:39:08.120
and this

00:39:08.120 --> 00:39:09.730
and all these intangibles,

00:39:09.730 --> 00:39:11.510
I think one of the things that's,

00:39:11.510 --> 00:39:13.380
oftentimes when I get
into a nerdy discussion

00:39:13.380 --> 00:39:14.940
about your career,

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:16.560
you already bought up a
couple times here today

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:18.320
which is why I'm smiling inside,

00:39:18.320 --> 00:39:20.458
the great, great-

00:39:20.458 --> 00:39:21.469
not misunderstanding,

00:39:21.469 --> 00:39:24.270
the number one thing I think
you're most underrated for

00:39:24.270 --> 00:39:27.210
was what you did inside the
locker room and your teammates,

00:39:27.210 --> 00:39:29.810
when who you were in the outside world.

00:39:29.810 --> 00:39:32.480
Without having the ability
to have the humility,

00:39:32.480 --> 00:39:35.630
and to understand the
weight you were carrying

00:39:35.630 --> 00:39:38.620
in the outside world,
inside the locker room,

00:39:38.620 --> 00:39:41.370
that is absolutely your legacy
as a leader, in my opinion.

00:39:41.370 --> 00:39:43.760
That's why you are who you are.

00:39:43.760 --> 00:39:45.600
I think Sam has that.

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:49.160
Not that he has the same
level of macro-fame,

00:39:49.160 --> 00:39:50.370
but if you do not know

00:39:50.370 --> 00:39:52.110
how to work a locker
room as a quarterback,

00:39:52.110 --> 00:39:54.143
and what you have to do off the field,

00:39:54.980 --> 00:39:58.930
I mean, this kid's studying film 24/7.

00:39:58.930 --> 00:39:59.997
I think we have a shot with this one.

00:39:59.997 --> 00:40:02.320
- You know, I agree of course, Gary,

00:40:02.320 --> 00:40:04.480
I watched him in college too at SC,

00:40:04.480 --> 00:40:07.470
and he looked like he had the
physical tools and all that,

00:40:07.470 --> 00:40:10.530
but during the coarse
of the season last year,

00:40:10.530 --> 00:40:14.400
hearing his coaches,
especially his teammates-

00:40:14.400 --> 00:40:16.160
- Josh McCown.
- Talk about him,

00:40:16.160 --> 00:40:18.250
and how they talked about him,

00:40:18.250 --> 00:40:21.662
that left a better
impression, even, you know?

00:40:21.662 --> 00:40:23.310
- I agree.
- And getting to meet him,

00:40:23.310 --> 00:40:26.820
and just visiting briefly
a few times with him,

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:27.860
really respect him.

00:40:27.860 --> 00:40:30.610
I like his size, he's bigger than I

00:40:30.610 --> 00:40:31.890
thought he was,

00:40:31.890 --> 00:40:33.610
and I can't complain.

00:40:33.610 --> 00:40:35.640
But you know, it's a team game.

00:40:35.640 --> 00:40:37.480
- [Both] It is a team game.

00:40:37.480 --> 00:40:38.610
- Speaking of team game,

00:40:38.610 --> 00:40:41.730
who's the most underrated
teammate you ever played with?

00:40:41.730 --> 00:40:42.620
Tight end?

00:40:42.620 --> 00:40:43.453
Guard?

00:40:43.453 --> 00:40:45.810
Somebody, obviously, there's
the names that we all know,

00:40:45.810 --> 00:40:46.890
but a little bit left field.

00:40:46.890 --> 00:40:48.655
Anybody stand out as

00:40:48.655 --> 00:40:51.620
"Wow, I wish more people realized",

00:40:51.620 --> 00:40:52.920
because what's amazing about sports,

00:40:52.920 --> 00:40:54.520
and it's the same as running a company,

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:58.320
it's subtle intangibles
that are very hard to see.

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:01.410
You know, whether it's picking
somebody up on the bench,

00:41:01.410 --> 00:41:03.040
whether its reinforcing something,

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:04.430
whether it's keeping somebody in line

00:41:04.430 --> 00:41:06.040
who was starting to be a cancer,

00:41:06.040 --> 00:41:07.300
is there anybody who stands out

00:41:07.300 --> 00:41:11.490
that even the hardest-core
Jet fans may not know?

00:41:11.490 --> 00:41:14.520
- I don't know about the
hardest-core Jet fans

00:41:14.520 --> 00:41:17.530
may not know, because there's
one guy on our offensive line

00:41:17.530 --> 00:41:20.770
who took a beating every week,
our center, John Schmidt.

00:41:20.770 --> 00:41:22.640
I mean, this was at a time that,

00:41:22.640 --> 00:41:24.830
it wasn't head slapping, forget about it,

00:41:24.830 --> 00:41:26.390
it wasn't head slapping, man,

00:41:26.390 --> 00:41:28.004
these cats were gonna hit
you right in the head.

00:41:28.004 --> 00:41:31.530
- I was watching Mean Joe
Green's, this weekend,

00:41:31.530 --> 00:41:34.290
NFL Life, with my son Xander,
did I introduce you two?

00:41:34.290 --> 00:41:35.790
I'm trying to educate him on the game,

00:41:35.790 --> 00:41:36.700
looking at those highlights,

00:41:36.700 --> 00:41:37.970
it's crazy what they were doing!

00:41:37.970 --> 00:41:39.710
- Unbelievable.
- It was crazy!

00:41:39.710 --> 00:41:41.100
- And then the linemen had to block

00:41:41.100 --> 00:41:42.170
with their hands in here,

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:43.644
they couldn't even reach out-

00:41:43.644 --> 00:41:44.600
- They were just
- Keep cats away from them.

00:41:44.600 --> 00:41:45.433
- Taking a beating.

00:41:45.433 --> 00:41:46.863
- The offensive linemen,

00:41:47.740 --> 00:41:51.390
and to this day, your games
mostly are won in the pits,

00:41:51.390 --> 00:41:52.250
- 100 percent,
- You know,

00:41:52.250 --> 00:41:53.500
- The trenches.
- You gotta have

00:41:53.500 --> 00:41:54.957
the guys up front on both sides

00:41:54.957 --> 00:41:56.120
- Facebook, YouTube,
- Of the ball.

00:41:56.120 --> 00:41:57.560
- I apologize, Joe, put
in your phone numbers,

00:41:57.560 --> 00:41:59.960
I'm gonna take a couple
calls and wrap it up.

00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:01.850
So, (exhales)

00:42:01.850 --> 00:42:03.980
what's really interesting about,

00:42:03.980 --> 00:42:06.450
how much did you love playing in Shea?

00:42:06.450 --> 00:42:07.840
Because Shea's got this aura,

00:42:07.840 --> 00:42:10.080
I didn't get to go to any games there,

00:42:10.080 --> 00:42:12.586
'cause they'd moved the
giant stadium at that point,

00:42:12.586 --> 00:42:14.840
there was something about it.

00:42:14.840 --> 00:42:17.706
- Well yeah, it was in
New York, for one thing.

00:42:17.706 --> 00:42:19.442
- (laughs) Yes.
- I mean, I'm not

00:42:19.442 --> 00:42:21.211
- I get it, I get it.
- Putting Jersey down at all,

00:42:21.211 --> 00:42:22.487
- It was also
- 'Cause those fans

00:42:22.487 --> 00:42:23.470
- A different time!
- Were supporters,

00:42:23.470 --> 00:42:25.950
but this was our team out here in Queens,

00:42:25.950 --> 00:42:28.860
you know, right there on the island there,

00:42:28.860 --> 00:42:33.860
and also we got the
practice later in September,

00:42:33.950 --> 00:42:36.540
because the Mets were there
and we shared it with Mets,

00:42:36.540 --> 00:42:38.820
so we got to know Tom and the guys-

00:42:38.820 --> 00:42:40.050
- Seaver.
- Whatever, yeah,

00:42:40.050 --> 00:42:43.459
But also we got to
practice at Rikers Island!

00:42:43.459 --> 00:42:44.800
(Gary laughs)
Rikers Island, as you know,

00:42:44.800 --> 00:42:45.775
is a prison!

00:42:45.775 --> 00:42:46.608
- Yep!
(crew laughs)

00:42:46.608 --> 00:42:49.580
- And that's the most memorable
place I remember practicing!

00:42:49.580 --> 00:42:51.650
- Joe, tell me about being
in New York during that time.

00:42:51.650 --> 00:42:54.410
You guys win, the Mets
win, which was improbable,

00:42:54.410 --> 00:42:55.950
the Knicks win a year later,

00:42:55.950 --> 00:42:58.180
did you have a relation
with Walt Clyde Frazier?

00:42:58.180 --> 00:43:00.177
- Oh, a nice relationship-

00:43:00.177 --> 00:43:01.440
- Did you guys hang?
- We didn't hang, no-

00:43:01.440 --> 00:43:03.550
- Okay, 'cause the two of you,
- He was working-

00:43:03.550 --> 00:43:05.210
- The two of you are legendary men

00:43:05.210 --> 00:43:06.760
off the field in New York City,

00:43:06.760 --> 00:43:07.950
and I secretly have always,

00:43:07.950 --> 00:43:09.350
I'm so glad I just got to ask you that,

00:43:09.350 --> 00:43:10.310
'cause if you'd left and I didn't ask,

00:43:10.310 --> 00:43:11.320
I would've been upset.

00:43:11.320 --> 00:43:13.197
I was like "Ooh man, I
wonder if the two of them

00:43:13.197 --> 00:43:14.340
"ever hit the town together."

00:43:14.340 --> 00:43:16.780
That must've been insanity.

00:43:16.780 --> 00:43:18.320
So you had respect for each
other, but you didn't hang.

00:43:18.320 --> 00:43:20.820
- Oh, I was pulling for
him, well it's those eyes,

00:43:20.820 --> 00:43:22.840
cold eyes, even, but I tell you what-

00:43:22.840 --> 00:43:23.673
- Did you go to any of
those championships?

00:43:23.673 --> 00:43:25.520
- Knicks games, I don't know,

00:43:25.520 --> 00:43:27.970
I was out of town during that time,

00:43:27.970 --> 00:43:29.770
but I tell you what,

00:43:29.770 --> 00:43:31.060
Roger Bert-

00:43:31.060 --> 00:43:31.893
- I was just gonna ask you,

00:43:31.893 --> 00:43:35.180
- Was the man that swears
he was at the suave,

00:43:35.180 --> 00:43:37.740
the suede, and he was the coolest cat.

00:43:37.740 --> 00:43:38.573
- Did you go to Ranger games?

00:43:38.573 --> 00:43:40.770
- No-
- Did you go to any games?

00:43:40.770 --> 00:43:42.060
Did you go to Met games?

00:43:42.060 --> 00:43:43.445
Did you do that,

00:43:43.445 --> 00:43:44.278
- I tell you what-
- Or did you just kind of

00:43:44.278 --> 00:43:45.111
stay in your own lane?

00:43:45.111 --> 00:43:46.630
- When the season was over,

00:43:46.630 --> 00:43:48.380
I usually

00:43:48.380 --> 00:43:49.720
left town.

00:43:49.720 --> 00:43:51.790
First of all, if you're in New York

00:43:51.790 --> 00:43:54.440
186 days a year, you are a resident,

00:43:54.440 --> 00:43:57.283
- I love it!
- And not that I kept up with,

00:43:58.192 --> 00:44:00.143
not that I kept up with it much, but-

00:44:01.270 --> 00:44:03.997
- The taxes are much better outside of it.

00:44:03.997 --> 00:44:06.295
- And having played-
- That's amazing, I love that.

00:44:06.295 --> 00:44:07.128
So you were that thoughtful about it back,

00:44:07.128 --> 00:44:10.210
like you were real purposeful,
that makes sense, I get it.

00:44:10.210 --> 00:44:13.340
- I only learned as I went
along, I didn't know that-

00:44:13.340 --> 00:44:14.173
- You were like "yes, sir".

00:44:14.173 --> 00:44:16.140
- But I knew I loved Florida,

00:44:16.140 --> 00:44:18.950
because of being there as
a sophomore in college,

00:44:18.950 --> 00:44:20.900
play a Bowl game against Oklahoma,

00:44:20.900 --> 00:44:22.258
playing Texas, okay-

00:44:22.258 --> 00:44:23.660
- The Super Bowl.
- Look at this place,

00:44:23.660 --> 00:44:26.623
- The Super Bowl.
- And yeah, okay, that too.

00:44:26.623 --> 00:44:28.680
So I knew I loved Florida,

00:44:28.680 --> 00:44:29.970
I loved the weather down there,

00:44:29.970 --> 00:44:31.760
I got to where I needed

00:44:31.760 --> 00:44:33.990
- So your off-season
- Blue skies instead of gray.

00:44:33.990 --> 00:44:35.240
- Was Florida, 'Bama,

00:44:35.240 --> 00:44:36.920
a little Pennsylvania or no?

00:44:36.920 --> 00:44:39.210
- Pennsylvania, my mother
and family are still,

00:44:39.210 --> 00:44:42.350
my mother's passed but my
family's still back there,

00:44:42.350 --> 00:44:45.180
they're survivors, but I
became a resident of Florida

00:44:45.180 --> 00:44:46.823
in 1970.

00:44:48.124 --> 00:44:49.230
- Ahead of your time, Joe.
- In 1970.

00:44:49.230 --> 00:44:50.595
- Impressive work.

00:44:50.595 --> 00:44:51.608
(phone rings)
Who is this?

00:44:51.608 --> 00:44:52.441
- Chase.
- Chase?

00:44:52.441 --> 00:44:57.441
- In Connecticut.
- You got it.

00:44:58.578 --> 00:45:01.231
- Well, I know Connecticut,
I don't know about-

00:45:01.231 --> 00:45:02.064
- [Chase] Hello?

00:45:02.064 --> 00:45:02.897
- Yeah!
- Hey, Chase,

00:45:02.897 --> 00:45:05.580
it's Gary Vaynerchuk, you're
on with the legend Joe Namath.

00:45:05.580 --> 00:45:06.430
- No way!
- You did it, Chase!

00:45:06.430 --> 00:45:08.620
- How's it going, guys?
- It's going well, Chase,

00:45:08.620 --> 00:45:09.870
how are you?

00:45:09.870 --> 00:45:11.320
- [Chase] Doing well!

00:45:11.320 --> 00:45:12.880
- You got a question?

00:45:12.880 --> 00:45:15.603
- [Chase] Yeah, so my
question for you is this:

00:45:16.610 --> 00:45:17.443
Joe,

00:45:20.414 --> 00:45:22.530
is there any similarities

00:45:22.530 --> 00:45:23.690
between

00:45:23.690 --> 00:45:26.250
being a football quarterback

00:45:26.250 --> 00:45:28.860
and being an entrepreneur.

00:45:28.860 --> 00:45:29.710
- [Gary] That's an interesting question,

00:45:29.710 --> 00:45:31.100
what do you think, Joe?

00:45:31.100 --> 00:45:32.560
- I know more about football

00:45:32.560 --> 00:45:35.330
than I do about being an entrepreneur

00:45:35.330 --> 00:45:37.530
and entrepreneurialship,

00:45:37.530 --> 00:45:40.640
but I think management is important

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:42.180
of whatever you're doing,

00:45:42.180 --> 00:45:45.420
and communicating with the
person there you're working with,

00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:48.140
yeah, there are similarities

00:45:48.140 --> 00:45:49.500
in life in general.

00:45:49.500 --> 00:45:52.590
- Joe, you also played in
the era where you were,

00:45:52.590 --> 00:45:54.340
correct me here, you were calling

00:45:54.340 --> 00:45:56.210
the plays at the line, right?

00:45:56.210 --> 00:45:58.293
- Yeah, we had the liberty
to do that, that's right-

00:45:58.293 --> 00:46:00.020
- [Gary] Did you take that liberty a lot?

00:46:00.020 --> 00:46:01.343
- And they still do have the liberty-

00:46:01.343 --> 00:46:03.420
- I know, I know.
- To do that, yeah,

00:46:03.420 --> 00:46:04.980
depending on who your coach is.

00:46:04.980 --> 00:46:07.060
- [Gary] Of course, of course.

00:46:07.060 --> 00:46:09.570
- I saw Phil Simms and Bill Belichick

00:46:09.570 --> 00:46:12.240
go at it over there on
the sides, and I knew-

00:46:12.240 --> 00:46:13.073
- [Gary] Parcels.

00:46:13.073 --> 00:46:15.010
- Phil Simms knew what
he was doing out there,

00:46:15.010 --> 00:46:16.010
but you know what?

00:46:16.010 --> 00:46:17.670
If the play doesn't work-

00:46:17.670 --> 00:46:18.920
- [Gary] You're accountable.

00:46:18.920 --> 00:46:20.700
- You gotta have a reason,

00:46:20.700 --> 00:46:22.490
Coach Ewbank, when I was a rookie,

00:46:22.490 --> 00:46:24.287
told us quarterbacks

00:46:24.287 --> 00:46:27.807
"If I send in a play,
you run what I send in,

00:46:27.807 --> 00:46:29.337
"unless you have a better play,

00:46:29.337 --> 00:46:30.940
"I want to hear the reason."

00:46:30.940 --> 00:46:33.393
You gotta have a reason
to change the play,

00:46:34.554 --> 00:46:35.600
so I did

00:46:35.600 --> 00:46:36.760
enjoy being able to,

00:46:36.760 --> 00:46:38.720
and we won the championship game,

00:46:38.720 --> 00:46:41.440
basically calling 80-plus
percent of our plays

00:46:41.440 --> 00:46:42.820
at the line of scrimmage.

00:46:42.820 --> 00:46:44.220
- A whole lot of running.

00:46:44.220 --> 00:46:45.053
- Well yeah-
- You saw it?

00:46:45.053 --> 00:46:46.656
- Offensive line and
- It was dominating.

00:46:46.656 --> 00:46:49.640
Matt Snell, I never saw anybody
play a game like Matt Snell.

00:46:49.640 --> 00:46:52.840
I mean, he not only ran
that ball, god knows,

00:46:52.840 --> 00:46:55.840
20-plus times or whatever,
gained so many yards,

00:46:55.840 --> 00:46:59.660
caught passes, but
covered on kickoffs, too!

00:46:59.660 --> 00:47:02.580
Ran down the field on kickoffs, man!

00:47:02.580 --> 00:47:03.413
- [Gary] That's how they did it.

00:47:03.413 --> 00:47:04.246
- It was a different era.

00:47:04.246 --> 00:47:06.130
- Chase, thanks for calling, brother.

00:47:06.130 --> 00:47:07.540
- [Chase] Thanks so much, guys.

00:47:07.540 --> 00:47:08.373
- You got it.

00:47:09.640 --> 00:47:11.693
What did you do the day
after the Super Bowl?

00:47:12.540 --> 00:47:13.373
- Oh...

00:47:13.373 --> 00:47:14.830
- [Gary] Did you guys
party hard that night,

00:47:14.830 --> 00:47:17.100
or were you kind of, what happened?

00:47:17.100 --> 00:47:19.873
- Didn't party hard, I
had my girlfriend with me,

00:47:20.730 --> 00:47:23.320
we were relieved and we were just-

00:47:23.320 --> 00:47:25.210
- Did you truly, I don't even know,

00:47:25.210 --> 00:47:27.920
at this point it's been
talked about so much,

00:47:27.920 --> 00:47:30.900
I can understand how it can
manipulate in one's brain,

00:47:30.900 --> 00:47:33.083
but if you can for a second try,

00:47:34.349 --> 00:47:37.820
do you think you understood
the magnitude of that win?

00:47:37.820 --> 00:47:41.470
- To me, I understood it
only as far as a goal,

00:47:41.470 --> 00:47:45.137
the World Championship, that's
what it says on that ring,

00:47:45.137 --> 00:47:46.700
(Gary laughs)
"the World Champion".

00:47:46.700 --> 00:47:49.540
It wasn't called the
Super Bowl at the time,

00:47:49.540 --> 00:47:50.790
and I've said this again,

00:47:50.790 --> 00:47:54.010
if you're in little league,
make them win a championship.

00:47:54.010 --> 00:47:56.840
On every level you go,
you wanna be the best,

00:47:56.840 --> 00:47:58.240
and that part of the team-

00:47:58.240 --> 00:47:59.640
- Joe, I have an interesting question.

00:47:59.640 --> 00:48:01.390
You were already kind of known, but

00:48:02.550 --> 00:48:04.650
did your life actually completely change

00:48:04.650 --> 00:48:05.600
after that game, though?

00:48:05.600 --> 00:48:07.050
Like when you were at an airport,

00:48:07.050 --> 00:48:10.620
or at a diner, no matter
where you were in the country,

00:48:10.620 --> 00:48:13.330
did you just completely see a big shift?

00:48:13.330 --> 00:48:14.480
- Yeah, absolutely-

00:48:14.480 --> 00:48:16.268
- Makes sense.
- I went out to San Francisco

00:48:16.268 --> 00:48:18.330
- Let's do one more.
- After that game,

00:48:18.330 --> 00:48:21.870
I was going on a USO tour to
the hospitals in the Far East,

00:48:21.870 --> 00:48:24.090
and there was a group of people

00:48:24.090 --> 00:48:26.830
when I got to the airport in San Francisco

00:48:26.830 --> 00:48:28.607
holding up signs and all that,

00:48:28.607 --> 00:48:30.600
"Broadway Joe", "Congratulations" (scats).

00:48:30.600 --> 00:48:33.760
- So you knew.
- Yeah, it shocked me,

00:48:33.760 --> 00:48:35.260
I mean, San Francisco.

00:48:35.260 --> 00:48:38.420
So that was the first thing I remember

00:48:38.420 --> 00:48:41.010
about really being
recognized for that game.

00:48:41.010 --> 00:48:41.900
- Did you ever have a conversation

00:48:41.900 --> 00:48:43.810
about the game with Unitas?

00:48:43.810 --> 00:48:44.850
I know he didn't start that game,

00:48:44.850 --> 00:48:45.810
- Johnny and I,
- He obviously came in later.

00:48:45.810 --> 00:48:48.300
- We got to visit at many charity events

00:48:48.300 --> 00:48:49.520
and golf outings-

00:48:49.520 --> 00:48:50.800
- You got to know him later?
- At night,

00:48:50.800 --> 00:48:53.330
just the two of us, sitting and drinking

00:48:53.330 --> 00:48:56.520
a little bit afterwards, we
never discussed the game.

00:48:56.520 --> 00:48:58.335
- Never!
- No, no, no,

00:48:58.335 --> 00:48:59.503
- No, I get it!
- It didn't need to be said,

00:48:59.503 --> 00:49:00.336
you know?

00:49:01.720 --> 00:49:03.070
The score said it all,

00:49:03.070 --> 00:49:06.510
and Johnny wasn't himself,
that's the other thing,

00:49:06.510 --> 00:49:07.803
before that game,

00:49:08.670 --> 00:49:10.970
in Fort Lauderdale,

00:49:10.970 --> 00:49:14.010
their defensive tackle, Lou Michaels,

00:49:14.010 --> 00:49:16.240
and Dan Sullivan, their offensive guard,

00:49:16.240 --> 00:49:17.850
and Jim Hudson and I, our safety,

00:49:17.850 --> 00:49:19.324
had dinner together.

00:49:19.324 --> 00:49:21.090
- Get out of here!
- I knew Lou Michaels

00:49:21.090 --> 00:49:23.940
'cause he and my brother Frank
were at Kentucky together,

00:49:23.940 --> 00:49:24.830
- [Gary] I love it.

00:49:24.830 --> 00:49:27.910
- And so I got to meet Lou when
they came home from college,

00:49:27.910 --> 00:49:31.350
I was maybe 13, 14 at the time,

00:49:31.350 --> 00:49:34.410
and so we decided to have dinner,

00:49:34.410 --> 00:49:37.530
and we're sitting at the
bar, waiting for a table,

00:49:37.530 --> 00:49:40.000
and Lou and I, you know, and Jim, Dan,

00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:41.040
we have a drink or two,

00:49:41.040 --> 00:49:43.110
and for some reason,

00:49:43.110 --> 00:49:45.740
I guess he was a defensive lineman,

00:49:45.740 --> 00:49:47.260
for some reason, Lou turned to me,

00:49:47.260 --> 00:49:49.457
says "You know what, Joe?

00:49:49.457 --> 00:49:51.908
"We gonna kick your ass this week."

00:49:51.908 --> 00:49:52.770
(both laugh)

00:49:52.770 --> 00:49:55.007
I said "Whoa, no, Lou,

00:49:55.007 --> 00:49:57.417
"come on, man!

00:49:57.417 --> 00:49:58.687
"Come on!" I said,

00:49:58.687 --> 00:50:00.697
"And besides, what do you know about it?

00:50:00.697 --> 00:50:02.285
"You're just a kicker!"

00:50:02.285 --> 00:50:03.168
(both laugh)

00:50:03.168 --> 00:50:04.300
Oh man!

00:50:04.300 --> 00:50:05.353
- He got coming.
- This Joe,

00:50:05.353 --> 00:50:07.440
this straight boy, he got uptight,

00:50:07.440 --> 00:50:09.500
he started to move, and 'bout that time

00:50:09.500 --> 00:50:11.440
a guy came over with a tuxedo,

00:50:11.440 --> 00:50:14.060
and said "Table's ready,
guys", or whatever.

00:50:14.060 --> 00:50:16.280
So we got along the rest
of the night all right,

00:50:16.280 --> 00:50:17.890
but there was some chop-busting.

00:50:17.890 --> 00:50:19.470
- Obviously Johnny didn't start the game-

00:50:19.470 --> 00:50:21.480
- [Joe] He was hurt, that's
what I wanted to say.

00:50:21.480 --> 00:50:22.313
- I know that.

00:50:22.313 --> 00:50:24.910
When he came in, because
he was this legend

00:50:24.910 --> 00:50:27.190
that I know that you looked up to,

00:50:27.190 --> 00:50:28.187
was there a part of you saying

00:50:28.187 --> 00:50:29.380
"Don't do it, don't come in." (laughs)

00:50:29.380 --> 00:50:32.517
- Absolutely, absolutely.
- You're like "Shit, please"-

00:50:32.517 --> 00:50:33.490
- I can see myself standing there

00:50:33.490 --> 00:50:35.550
on the sideline right now.

00:50:35.550 --> 00:50:38.495
I never asked the good
lord to help us win.

00:50:38.495 --> 00:50:39.560
(Gary laughs)
I didn't, really,

00:50:39.560 --> 00:50:41.240
we always prayed for heath,

00:50:41.240 --> 00:50:44.300
and we always prayed to stay
healthy and not have injuries,

00:50:44.300 --> 00:50:46.690
but not to help us win the game.

00:50:46.690 --> 00:50:49.370
When Johnny came out there,
I looked up at the cluck,

00:50:49.370 --> 00:50:52.806
and I looked up to god
and I said "Please, God,

00:50:52.806 --> 00:50:55.533
(Gary laughs)
"let that clock run out."

00:50:55.533 --> 00:50:57.290
(laughs) You have no idea!

00:50:57.290 --> 00:50:58.510
But Johnny wasn't himself,

00:50:58.510 --> 00:50:59.810
- I know that.
- Like I told Lou,

00:50:59.810 --> 00:51:01.380
before I said "You're just a kicker",

00:51:01.380 --> 00:51:03.677
to Lou, I said "Lou, Johnny's hurt!

00:51:03.677 --> 00:51:05.937
"He can't throw the
ball 'cross the street.

00:51:05.937 --> 00:51:07.187
"And besides that, what do you know?

00:51:07.187 --> 00:51:08.275
"You're just a kicker."

00:51:08.275 --> 00:51:09.250
- I love it.
- Great.

00:51:09.250 --> 00:51:10.810
- We calling him?

00:51:10.810 --> 00:51:11.960
- Curtis.
- Curtis?

00:51:11.960 --> 00:51:12.793
- [Crew] Yep.

00:51:13.882 --> 00:51:16.473
(phone rings)

00:51:16.473 --> 00:51:17.306
- What if he's not home?
- Think Curtis Martin's

00:51:17.306 --> 00:51:18.923
calling us?
(both laugh)

00:51:18.923 --> 00:51:21.209
(phone rings)
- Then we're gonna move on.

00:51:21.209 --> 00:51:22.042
- And that's the first guy I thought of

00:51:22.042 --> 00:51:22.875
when you said Curtis,

00:51:22.875 --> 00:51:24.160
- Me too.
- Curtis Martin.

00:51:24.160 --> 00:51:24.993
- [Gary] What a gentleman, huh?

00:51:24.993 --> 00:51:27.422
- Oh man, class act, great player.

00:51:27.422 --> 00:51:28.323
(noise over phone)
- Curtis?

00:51:29.310 --> 00:51:30.597
- Yes.
- It's Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:51:30.597 --> 00:51:32.647
and you're on with the legend Joe Namath.

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:34.858
- [Curtis] Oh, holy crap!

00:51:34.858 --> 00:51:36.087
(laughs)
Hey, Gary V.!

00:51:36.087 --> 00:51:37.183
- How're you doing, Curtis?
- Hey, Joe!

00:51:39.123 --> 00:51:39.956
- [Curtis] Good.

00:51:39.956 --> 00:51:41.450
My question is this:

00:51:41.450 --> 00:51:42.993
In regards to,

00:51:44.350 --> 00:51:45.520
you know,

00:51:45.520 --> 00:51:48.930
transitioning from professional
sports at your time

00:51:48.930 --> 00:51:51.100
to afterwards, what do you think

00:51:51.100 --> 00:51:53.203
the major difference is now,

00:51:53.203 --> 00:51:54.370
- That's a good question.
- When transitioning

00:51:54.370 --> 00:51:56.257
from a pro athlete to whatever,

00:51:56.257 --> 00:51:57.950
- Big difference.
- 'Cause I feel like

00:51:57.950 --> 00:51:59.690
there's a lot of issues going on

00:51:59.690 --> 00:52:01.570
in regards to

00:52:01.570 --> 00:52:02.980
finances.

00:52:02.980 --> 00:52:04.750
- You know what, Curtis-
- Thank you, Curtis.

00:52:04.750 --> 00:52:07.867
- In this day and age, there's no excuse.

00:52:07.867 --> 00:52:10.610
"The player didn't want to listen."

00:52:10.610 --> 00:52:13.170
They're taught, maybe not from home,

00:52:13.170 --> 00:52:14.860
but from their high school coaches,

00:52:14.860 --> 00:52:16.360
from their college coaches,

00:52:16.360 --> 00:52:19.000
their mentors, they're taught
better than ever before

00:52:19.000 --> 00:52:23.410
to prepare for the time after
your sports playing days

00:52:23.410 --> 00:52:24.730
are over,

00:52:24.730 --> 00:52:29.350
so if it's bad luck, or they
weren't decent listeners,

00:52:29.350 --> 00:52:31.990
the players today are prepared better

00:52:31.990 --> 00:52:34.120
for everything outside of football,

00:52:34.120 --> 00:52:38.030
plus the nutrition they
have, the training they have,

00:52:38.030 --> 00:52:40.490
because of the game's growth,

00:52:40.490 --> 00:52:42.557
and people looking back and saying

00:52:42.557 --> 00:52:44.632
"How can we do this better?",

00:52:44.632 --> 00:52:46.700
there shouldn't be an excuse,

00:52:46.700 --> 00:52:47.997
but, you now,

00:52:50.110 --> 00:52:51.280
some guys just

00:52:52.570 --> 00:52:54.396
don't understand.

00:52:54.396 --> 00:52:56.820
- (stammers)

00:52:56.820 --> 00:52:58.020
Gonna wrap up on a little more

00:52:58.020 --> 00:53:00.300
Jets nerddom word association.

00:53:00.300 --> 00:53:02.500
Were you following during
Kenny O'Brien's years?

00:53:02.500 --> 00:53:03.530
- Yes, absolutely.
- What did you think

00:53:03.530 --> 00:53:04.660
of Kenny as a player?

00:53:04.660 --> 00:53:05.690
- I think Kenny

00:53:06.640 --> 00:53:09.530
was glad he had Wesley Walker. (laughs)

00:53:09.530 --> 00:53:11.390
- Sure was, and Al Toon.

00:53:11.390 --> 00:53:13.250
- [Joe] And Kenny was an ace.

00:53:13.250 --> 00:53:14.660
He was a heck of a passer-

00:53:14.660 --> 00:53:17.290
- His offensive line
situation was real difficult.

00:53:17.290 --> 00:53:18.680
- Well, that's where it starts, too,

00:53:18.680 --> 00:53:21.330
and offensively, and I've
said this many times,

00:53:21.330 --> 00:53:25.330
the first Manning to be in
the Pro Football Hall of Fame

00:53:25.330 --> 00:53:30.100
wouldn't be Peyton and it may
not be Eli if he gets there,

00:53:30.100 --> 00:53:32.260
it would be their dad, Archie!

00:53:32.260 --> 00:53:34.420
Archie never had a team to play with.

00:53:34.420 --> 00:53:35.890
- 100 percent.
- He played with

00:53:35.890 --> 00:53:37.730
the New Orleans Aint's.

00:53:37.730 --> 00:53:40.640
Not the Saints, they used to
put bags over their heads,

00:53:40.640 --> 00:53:41.870
- They were terrible.
- The fans did.

00:53:41.870 --> 00:53:45.750
So Archie was a great quarterback,
but didn't have the team.

00:53:45.750 --> 00:53:48.880
Dan Marino, he's in the Hall of Fame,

00:53:48.880 --> 00:53:51.740
but didn't have a team to
win, to get over the hump,

00:53:51.740 --> 00:53:54.990
to win a championship,
could Dan lead a team?

00:53:54.990 --> 00:53:56.030
- Yeah, of course.
- Yeah,

00:53:56.030 --> 00:53:59.250
give him San Francisco's
team, give him our team, yeah.

00:53:59.250 --> 00:54:01.010
- Yeah, of course.
- So, you know,

00:54:01.010 --> 00:54:02.320
lady luck plays a role.

00:54:02.320 --> 00:54:03.940
- What about that defensive line,

00:54:03.940 --> 00:54:07.367
that sack exchange, the
Kleckos, the Salaams,

00:54:07.367 --> 00:54:08.587
the Gastineaus, what did you think about-

00:54:08.587 --> 00:54:10.360
- And Marty Lyons, too,
- Those characters.

00:54:10.360 --> 00:54:11.790
- Yeah, absolutely.
- Of course.

00:54:11.790 --> 00:54:14.020
- Klecko, you're talking about a guy

00:54:14.020 --> 00:54:15.520
that should be in the Hall of Fame,

00:54:15.520 --> 00:54:17.060
- 100 percent.
- In my opinion.

00:54:17.060 --> 00:54:18.440
- Made a Pro Bowl in
three different positions.

00:54:18.440 --> 00:54:20.980
- Three different positions, you're right,

00:54:20.980 --> 00:54:22.820
and how can that be done?

00:54:22.820 --> 00:54:25.550
How can he not be in the Hall of Fame?

00:54:25.550 --> 00:54:28.460
I question that, but
he's in my Hall of Fame,

00:54:28.460 --> 00:54:30.590
and everybody, anybody that's ever played

00:54:30.590 --> 00:54:33.570
against Joe Klecko will back that up.

00:54:33.570 --> 00:54:34.413
- Joe, the book.

00:54:36.430 --> 00:54:38.160
There's been a couple books with you,

00:54:38.160 --> 00:54:40.790
the documentary was unbelievable,

00:54:40.790 --> 00:54:43.540
I watched that opening night,
and then many times over,

00:54:44.890 --> 00:54:47.020
is there a story out there that,

00:54:47.020 --> 00:54:48.323
even at this point,

00:54:49.881 --> 00:54:50.714
I always wonder about this

00:54:50.714 --> 00:54:52.460
because I think all of us as humans,

00:54:52.460 --> 00:54:54.389
I share a lot about my life,

00:54:54.389 --> 00:54:56.500
I document, I do a lot of stuff,

00:54:56.500 --> 00:54:57.970
but I always wonder about the stories

00:54:57.970 --> 00:55:00.700
that haven't been told,
or the underrated stories.

00:55:00.700 --> 00:55:01.533
At this point in your life,

00:55:01.533 --> 00:55:03.970
is there any stone that
hasn't been turned?

00:55:03.970 --> 00:55:04.820
Is there anything

00:55:06.297 --> 00:55:07.130
like your acting career,

00:55:07.130 --> 00:55:08.774
is there anything,

00:55:08.774 --> 00:55:09.607
do you ever think about stuff

00:55:09.607 --> 00:55:10.480
that you haven't talked about yet,

00:55:10.480 --> 00:55:12.840
that you say "Will I
ever talk about that?",

00:55:12.840 --> 00:55:13.720
'cause we all go

00:55:14.630 --> 00:55:16.960
and hold a couple things
that we never share,

00:55:16.960 --> 00:55:18.660
but are there things that you've
been thinking about sharing

00:55:18.660 --> 00:55:20.760
that maybe you haven't shared in the past?

00:55:20.760 --> 00:55:21.790
- No.
(everyone laughs)

00:55:21.790 --> 00:55:23.270
No, you know what?

00:55:23.270 --> 00:55:24.630
I have shared a lot-

00:55:24.630 --> 00:55:27.320
- You have, a ton.
- And I have to duck things,

00:55:27.320 --> 00:55:29.330
starting at home, stuff starts at home,

00:55:29.330 --> 00:55:32.117
and you take the responsibility
for your acts and all,

00:55:32.117 --> 00:55:34.490
and you try to make improvements on it,

00:55:34.490 --> 00:55:35.927
but some things, you know,

00:55:35.927 --> 00:55:38.750
are just not right being said,

00:55:38.750 --> 00:55:40.278
what's it gonna do?

00:55:40.278 --> 00:55:41.700
- I get it.
- Is it gonna harm somebody?

00:55:41.700 --> 00:55:43.870
Is it gonna hurt somebody
here or whatever?

00:55:43.870 --> 00:55:45.370
Is it gonna be positive?

00:55:45.370 --> 00:55:46.243
This book,

00:55:47.207 --> 00:55:48.780
a really important part of it

00:55:48.780 --> 00:55:51.740
is reaching out, trying to help,

00:55:51.740 --> 00:55:55.930
underdogs that feel
like they're underdogs,

00:55:55.930 --> 00:55:56.763
you know,

00:55:56.763 --> 00:55:59.050
I keep saying if you have a passion,

00:55:59.050 --> 00:56:01.470
go for it, try it, don't
let anybody tell you

00:56:01.470 --> 00:56:03.460
you cannot do something.

00:56:03.460 --> 00:56:05.670
If you have a problem with substance,

00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:08.010
whether it be drinking, drugs, whatever,

00:56:08.010 --> 00:56:09.483
reach out for help.

00:56:10.640 --> 00:56:12.530
When I was married,

00:56:12.530 --> 00:56:17.530
my former wife asked me
to quit drinking in 1986,

00:56:17.840 --> 00:56:22.120
and I stopped for
13-and-a-half years, all right?

00:56:22.120 --> 00:56:24.820
Then I found a reason
to go back and drink.

00:56:24.820 --> 00:56:27.293
I drank for a few more years,

00:56:29.387 --> 00:56:32.720
and then the Suzy Kolber
incident changed my life.

00:56:32.720 --> 00:56:34.630
She helped, I called her the next day

00:56:34.630 --> 00:56:39.270
when I found out what I had
done that night, I called her,

00:56:39.270 --> 00:56:41.300
in fact Jimmy called
me and told me about it

00:56:41.300 --> 00:56:42.730
the next day, I didn't know.

00:56:42.730 --> 00:56:44.310
- I understand.
- So I called her,

00:56:44.310 --> 00:56:47.810
and I apologized to her,
and she was so supportive.

00:56:47.810 --> 00:56:48.920
She

00:56:48.920 --> 00:56:51.610
inspired me more than

00:56:51.610 --> 00:56:53.460
I was inspired at the time,

00:56:53.460 --> 00:56:56.690
but I knew I let a lot of people
down, including my family,

00:56:56.690 --> 00:56:59.390
friends, you know, I needed help.

00:56:59.390 --> 00:57:01.720
I needed help, so I went and got help.

00:57:01.720 --> 00:57:04.270
I went to a facility, I got help,

00:57:04.270 --> 00:57:06.530
I got an education, I liked to call it.

00:57:06.530 --> 00:57:09.100
I wanted to understand
why this was happening,

00:57:09.100 --> 00:57:11.800
- You were listening.
- In retrospect,

00:57:11.800 --> 00:57:14.090
you know, it just became a way of life-

00:57:14.090 --> 00:57:15.200
- [Gary] I understand.

00:57:16.940 --> 00:57:20.630
- I got an education at
a professional facility,

00:57:20.630 --> 00:57:23.810
then I started going to A.A.
and still run across people

00:57:23.810 --> 00:57:26.540
and still do see people come over to me

00:57:26.540 --> 00:57:27.843
and they kind of whisper:

00:57:28.747 --> 00:57:31.080
"Joe, I'm a friend of Bill's."

00:57:31.080 --> 00:57:32.436
Well, you don't have to whisper!

00:57:32.436 --> 00:57:33.269
(both laugh)

00:57:33.269 --> 00:57:34.980
So am I, you know what I mean!

00:57:34.980 --> 00:57:36.630
Yeah.

00:57:36.630 --> 00:57:39.770
- We grew up in an era where
there was a stigma around that,

00:57:39.770 --> 00:57:42.800
and anytime we as a culture
can eliminate stigma,

00:57:42.800 --> 00:57:43.870
whether it's alcoholism,

00:57:43.870 --> 00:57:46.700
right now I think the stigma
of the next half-decade,

00:57:46.700 --> 00:57:49.830
next two, three decades,
hopefully is mental health issues,

00:57:49.830 --> 00:57:51.840
that we can start eliminating this stigma

00:57:51.840 --> 00:57:54.000
to allow people to feel comfortable,

00:57:54.000 --> 00:57:55.530
because other people's judgment

00:57:55.530 --> 00:57:57.090
dictates a lot of our behavior.

00:57:57.090 --> 00:57:59.303
- Yes, yes, and addiction,

00:58:00.700 --> 00:58:03.800
if it can be addiction to
the good lord and love, okay,

00:58:03.800 --> 00:58:05.940
but many of the times we talk addiction,

00:58:05.940 --> 00:58:07.289
it's negative, you know?

00:58:07.289 --> 00:58:08.909
- That's right, that's right.
- And, so,

00:58:08.909 --> 00:58:11.780
if you get caught in
that kind of a thought,

00:58:11.780 --> 00:58:13.510
if you think you've got a problem,

00:58:13.510 --> 00:58:15.700
you probably do, and
you need to reach out,

00:58:15.700 --> 00:58:18.440
'cause there are people
that want to help you.

00:58:18.440 --> 00:58:19.730
- Joe, it's been an honor to have you.

00:58:19.730 --> 00:58:20.563
- [Joe] Thank you, Gary.

00:58:20.563 --> 00:58:21.780
- One of the fun things on this show

00:58:21.780 --> 00:58:25.260
is the guest gets to ask
the question of the day.

00:58:25.260 --> 00:58:28.210
So I'm gonna give you a
few seconds here to think.

00:58:28.210 --> 00:58:30.610
You've got thousands, tens of thousands,

00:58:30.610 --> 00:58:32.370
hundreds of thousands over the next month

00:58:32.370 --> 00:58:33.600
that are gonna watch this.

00:58:33.600 --> 00:58:36.150
What question do you
want to ask the masses?

00:58:36.150 --> 00:58:39.180
What do you want them to think
about, what kind of question?

00:58:39.180 --> 00:58:40.223
- Wow.

00:58:42.592 --> 00:58:44.038
- Are you curious about,
- The big issue right now,

00:58:44.038 --> 00:58:45.610
- Please, please.
- I've been wrestling with

00:58:45.610 --> 00:58:48.430
is trying to understand
this abortion issue.

00:58:48.430 --> 00:58:49.440
- [Gary] Interesting.

00:58:49.440 --> 00:58:51.420
- And, you know,

00:58:51.420 --> 00:58:52.720
I believe in God,

00:58:52.720 --> 00:58:54.070
I believe in religion,

00:58:54.070 --> 00:58:56.720
I also believe in the right for a lady

00:58:56.720 --> 00:58:59.890
at a time to have a choice

00:58:59.890 --> 00:59:00.723
on what's going on.

00:59:00.723 --> 00:59:03.270
How're we gonna get that straightened out?

00:59:03.270 --> 00:59:04.287
- [Gary] Starting in Alabama,
too, interesting place.

00:59:04.287 --> 00:59:06.133
- How're we gonna get
this straightened out?

00:59:06.133 --> 00:59:07.530
- It's a powerful question, Joe.

00:59:07.530 --> 00:59:08.540
Thanks for being on the show.

00:59:08.540 --> 00:59:09.800
- [Joe] All right, thank you, Gary.

00:59:09.800 --> 00:59:10.633
- You got it.

00:59:10.633 --> 00:59:14.240
You keep asking questions,
we'll keep answering them.

00:59:14.240 --> 00:59:16.990
On this episode, my favorite human being

00:59:16.990 --> 00:59:18.803
in the world stops by.

00:59:19.887 --> 00:59:20.763
(slaps leg)

00:59:20.763 --> 00:59:22.120
(crew claps)
- [Joe] Aw man.

00:59:22.120 --> 00:59:23.171
- [Gary] That was fun.

00:59:23.171 --> 00:59:24.004
(crew chatters)

